\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-India's patchy rupee defense to slow, not halt, decline\b0\par\par\uc2 By Saikat Chatterjee and Aditya Phatak  \par \uc2 981 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Dezember 2011\par 09:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Dec 20 (Reuters) - India's piecemeal steps to defend the rupee can do little more than slow its decline for now, and some of the measures risk hurting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the long term.\par \par \uc2 In particular, new \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rules that reduce trading limits of Indian banks and companies could have the unintended effect of hampering their ability to hedge, choking off market liquidity and driving a chunk of trading to offshore markets.\par \par \uc2 The rupee's weakness -- a drop of nearly 20 percent from July highs to a record low last week -- reflects the increasing doubts of foreign investors over the ability of India to tame high inflation, prop up falling growth and rein-in its large fiscal and current account deficits.\par \par \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  expects rupee trading volumes to drop by 25 to 30 percent after measures implemented late last week.\par \par \uc2 Thinning liquidity could amplify volatility, dragging the rupee beyond last week's record low if bearishness morphs into a full-fledged selloff.\par \par \uc2 "Structurally, it will reduce liquidity and this will mean more volatility in the long run, but this is acceptable as the move reduces pressure for now," said Dariusz Kowalczyk, senior economist at \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 Restrictions in the onshore market could also drive more flows to the flourishing offshore non-deliverable forwards market, where rupee volumes are exceeded in Asia only by the Korean won and the Chinese yuan.\par \par \uc2 A senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a European Bank in Mumbai said onshore banks would be wary of trading large volumes with their clients on worries they would not be able to offset their exposure.\par \par \uc2 The best hope for the rupee, traders and economists argue, is for India to get its fiscal and economic house in order.\par \par \uc2 "As long as India's fundamentals remain weak, these various capital controls will not help the rupee in the longer term," said Ila Patnaik, an economist at the National Institute of Public Finance and Policy in Mumbai.\par \par \uc2 "By restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, the RBI (\uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2 ) is sending a wrong message out to market participants, especially companies with genuine needs," she said.\par \par \uc2 The RBI has taken a slew of measures to stem the rupee's fall, including raising quotas for foreigners to buy local debt, encouraging domestic firms to borrow more overseas, and even \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, something it does only in order to ease volatility.\par \par \uc2 Late on Friday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted the cap on interest rates local banks can pay non-resident Indians on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits, which might attract short term inflows but exposes India to volatile hot money.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the rupee sank to a record low 54.30 to the dollar, down almost a fifth from its July highs, making it the year's worst performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia.\par \par \uc2 It got a bounce on Friday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  took measures to curb speculation but it has resumed its decline since then, with market players saying the only way to improve the outlook for the rupee is to turn around a flagging economy.\par \par \uc2 "These are measures which will definitely reduce speculation from the market but what will it do is make the market shallow," said the head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at an Indian bank in Mumbai.\par \par \uc2 "Desirable or not desirable, it has shown results. This is the most unsophisticated way of doing things and a very crude way of handling things," said the trader, who declined to be identified.\par \par \uc2 PRESSURE PERSISTS\par \par \uc2 India is sitting on more than $300 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves but that is not as big a pile as it looks given the need to fund a widening current account deficit and a worsening fiscal and economic growth outlook.\par \par \uc2 Because the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is neither able nor willing to mount a major market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it is expected to continue using assorted administrative measures in hopes of spurring inflows and boosting confidence.\par \par \uc2 Pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to persist. The rupee was trading at 53.03 on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Australia's \uc2 Westpac Banking Corp\uc2  has made shorting the rupee against the dollar one of its top trade recommendations for 2012, while \uc2 UBS\uc2  expects a test of 55 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Jamal Mecklai, who heads \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  brokerage Mecklai Financial Services, said in an editorial in the Financial Express on Monday that the move to restrict trading positions "will squeeze liquidity and make risk management much more difficult, will achieve precious little in terms of curbing speculative demand."\par \par \uc2 GHOSTS OF CRISES PAST\par \par \uc2 Past efforts to prop up the rupee are hardly encouraging. \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's last large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a move in 2007 to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by flooding the money market with cash and bringing short-term interest rates to near zero, sparked inflation.\par \par \uc2 An April 2011 paper by Patnaik and fellow economist Ajay Shah on the experience of Indian policymakers with capital controls found them largely ineffective. (For the full report, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/mam65s"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/mam65s }}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 If downward pressure on the rupee persists, the RBI could offer more incentives to exporters, such as subsidising interest fees and allowing short-term money to enter the local bond markets without any restrictions.\par \par \uc2 It could revert to measures last seen during the Asian financial crisis, when India charged punitive interest rates on overdue export bills.\par \par \uc2 The RBI also raised policy interest rates at that time, an option that is off the table now as growth in the economy slows after 13 rate increases since early 2010.\par \par \uc2 "The structural problems like the current account deficit, trade deficit and a weakening economic growth trajectory remain bothersome for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and solutions to these need to be found soon," said N.S. Venkatesh, treasurer at \uc2 IDBI Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Tony Munroe and Ted Kerr)\par \par \uc2 INDIA/RUPEE (ANALYSIS)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111220e7ck000ce\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
