\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Rupee Hits New All-Time Low For Fourth Straight Day\b0\par\par\uc2 258 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 2011\par 04:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Asian Equities Report\par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 )--The Indian rupee continued its free fall against the U.S. dollar Thursday, sinking to a new record low for the fourth straight day, as investors fled risk-sensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to escalating concerns over Europe's sovereign debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 The dollar raced past its previous record high of INR53.88, reached Wednesday, and was quoted at INR54.21 at 0338 GMT after rising to INR54.22 earlier.\par \par \uc2 Global risk sentiment took a hit after the euro fell below the key psychological threshold of $1.30 Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The dollar has gained more than 21% against the rupee since March, making the local unit Asia's worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year. Risk-aversion stemming from the euro-zone crisis has magnified concerns over India's high domestic inflation, slowing economic growth and the possibility of the budget deficit widening.\par \par \uc2 The rupee's tumble has increased the clamor for action from authorities, with two senior advisers to the prime minister Wednesday calling for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 While the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  has maintained that it will only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to manage volatility, analysts expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to announce some measures to boost dollar liquidity at its mid-quarter monetary polic