\par\par \b \uc2 India cbank sold dollars in  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  mkt for a second month in Oct\b0\par\par\uc2 396 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 2011\par 12:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Dec 12 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in October, for a second consecutive month, after the Indian rupee came under withering selling pressure on deepening domestic and global growth concerns, its monthly bulletin showed on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India (RBI) \uc2 has always maintained that it does not target any specific exchange rate on the rupee and only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent excessive volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 The rupee had fallen to as low as 50.32 per dollar on Oct. 21, and is down nearly 17 percent from its July peak this year. The unit touched a record low of 52.73 on Nov. 22 and losing 7 percent in the month, its worst fall in 16 years.\par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the worst performing unit against the dollar among major Asian peers this year and investors continue to stay bearish on the rupee, a Reuters poll last week showed.\par \par \uc2 In fact, India may face its worst financial crisis in decades if it fails to stem a slide in the rupee, leaving the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a difficult choice over how to make best use of its limited reserves to maintain the confidence of foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 RBI sold $943 million in October, but did not buy any dollars, as in the preceding September, where it had sold $845 million dollars and not purchased any, the bulletin showed.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar sales in September had been its first after November 2010, RBI data showed.\par \par \uc2 On a net basis, November 2010 was the RBI's biggest monthly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since June 2009, when it had bought $1.04 billion.\par \par \uc2 In 2010, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought a net $1.85 billion, compared with net sales of $5.8 billion in 2009 when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the rupee from depreciating sharply.\par \par \uc2 At 4:29 p.m. on Monday, the partially convertible rupee\par \par \uc2 was at 52.64/65 per dollar, 1.15 percent weaker than its Friday's close of 52.03/04. Earlier in the day, it had dropped to 52.6850 against the dollar, close to its record low before being pulled up by possible dollar sales from some nationalised banks.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Aditya Phatak; Editing by Harish Nambiar)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INDIA\b0 \highlight\uc2 -CBANK-\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 /   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111212e7cc000n3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
