\par\par \b \uc2 Rupee volatility to remain till eurozone crisis resolved : cbank chief\b0\par\par\uc2 159 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2011\par 09:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 CHANDIGARH, India, Nov 25 (Reuters) - Volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will remain until the euro zone crisis is resolved, India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Duvvuri Subbarao said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Subbarao said in a speech in the north-western city of Chandigarh that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is watching the rupee, but could not say whether it will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market directly.\par \par \uc2 The rupee has skidded nearly 17 percent from a 2011 high reached in late July as risk-averse investors flee emerging markets, increasing the difficulties for a government already struggling with high inflation, slowing economic growth and a widening trade gap.\par \par \uc2 The rupee touched an all-time low of 52.73 on Tuesday and state-run banks were spotted selling dollars in the market in recent sessions, sparking talk of RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Rajesh Kumar Singh; Editing by Subhadip Sircar)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INDIA\b0 \highlight\uc2 /CBANK/RUPEE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111128e7bp000f3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
