\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  Central Banker: To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  In  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Market Only To Manage Excess Volatility\b0\par\par\uc2 556 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2011\par 11:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --RBI Governor says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  watching rupee's movement\par \par \uc2 --Have taken some action, but can't say directly if will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Subbarao\par \par \uc2 --Allowing foreign investment in retail important for controlling inflation\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   (Updates \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  level, adds comments in 2nd-3rd, 10th, 12th and 13th paragraphs, background in 4th, 6th-8th paragraphs, analyst's comment in 9th paragraph) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Anant Vijay Kala and Sudeep Jain\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CHANDIGARH, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (Dow Jones)--\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets only to calm excess volatility and ensure macroeconomic stability, its chief said Friday, reiterating the known \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  view.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There was softening in the rupee yesterday. The Reserve Bank is watching the rupee's movement. We have taken some action. Whether we will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market or not, I can't tell you directly," RBI Governor Duvvuri Subbarao told reporters in Chandigarh.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He said also that the exchange rate could move both ways in the short-term.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Global factors are influencing the rupee's movement, and exchange rate volatility is likely to continue until the euro-zone's economic troubles ease, Subbarao added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Indian rupee--Asia's worst-performing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in 2011--has fallen about 17% against the U.S. dollar since April, battered by a wave of global risk aversion unleashed by the euro zone's sovereign debt problems, coupled with concerns over high domestic inflation and slowing economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar touched its all-time high of INR52.725 against the rupee Tuesday, and was quoting at INR52.36 at 1016 GMT Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In recent days, Indian authorities have swung into action to attract long-term capital inflows and increase the supply of dollars to bolster the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The RBI Wednesday eased rules on overseas borrowing by Indian firms, relaxed regulations on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swap hedges by companies and also raised the cap on interest rates banks can pay non-resident Indian depositors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Thursday, the federal cabinet lifted a ban on multi-brand foreign retailers, allowing them to own up to 51% of their Indian joint ventures. It also removed a 51% investment cap on single-brand foreign retailers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The move to ease foreign investment in retail is positive for sentiment in the rupee, although the impact will only be felt in the medium-term, said J. Moses Harding, executive vice president at \f28 \uc2 \f2 IndusInd Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Subbarao Friday said the government's move on foreign investment in retail is important for controlling inflation and increasing overall growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's inflation, which has remained above 9% for the 11 consecutive months through October, leaves little room for the RBI to ease its tight policy stance, despite weakening economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The country needs to bring inflation down to 5%--and even lower--and reduce budget deficits at the federal level and in states to ensure macroeconomic stability, Subbarao said Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the RBI's monetary tightening has been aimed at restraining demand and anchoring inflation expectations, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's policy stance has to be supplemented by supply side responses to raise the potential output of the economy, he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   --By Anant Vijay Kala and Sudeep Jain, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 6145 6123; sudeep.jain@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 25-11-11 1021GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111125e7bp000n1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
