\par\par \b \uc2 India cash rates end higher on high demand, liquidity stays tight\b0\par\par\uc2 428 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 2011\par 09:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Nov 24 (Reuters) - Indian cash rates ended well above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's repo rate on Thursday as demand from banks continued to be high and tightness in liquidity persisted.\par \par \uc2 The one-day cash rate ended at 8.70/8.75 percent, compared with Wednesday's close of 8.40/8.45 percent.\par \par \uc2 Banks borrowed 1.13 trillion rupees from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's liquidity adjustment facility window, slightly lower compared with 1.35 trillion rupees on Wednesday, but significantly higher than at the end of the first week of November.\par \par \uc2 Funds remained stretched in the banking system pressured by heavy government bond supply and banks' need for meeting cash reserve requirement on additional deposit growth.\par \par \uc2 Traders said till the rupee depreciation halts, the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  will not be able to ease liquidity.\par \par \uc2 Dollar selling by the RBI to stem the rupee's slide sucks out rupee liquidity adding to an already tight cash situation.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is suspected to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the second day in a row, nudging the rupee a tad higher.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth sharp movements in the rupee and prevent a downward spiral in its value, but will balance this with the need to retain reserves in the event of prolonged turbulence, deputy governor Subir Gokarn said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Indian bank deposits were up 17.5 percent from a year earlier, latest data from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in a bid to ease tight banking system liquidity, bought back 94.35 billion rupees of bonds in the first of its kind \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  this fiscal year that began April 1.\par \par \uc2 Volumes in the call market were at 121.68 billion rupees compared with a total of 101.85 billion rupees on Wednesday, Clearing Corp of India data showed.\par \par \uc2 Volumes in the CBLO market were 364.03 billion rupees, as against a total of 366.22 billion rupees on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The weighted average rate in the call money market and in the CBLO market were steady at 8.72 percent and 8.49 percent respectively from Wednesday's close.\par \par \uc2 In the inter-bank repo market, volumes were 86.79 billion rupees versus a total of 167.36 billion rupees on Wednesday and the weighted average rate was at 8.62 percent from 8.65 percent previously.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Shamik Paul and Aditya Phatak; Editing by Subhadip Sircar)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-CALL/(UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111127e7bo0005v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
