\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Factors behind the Indian rupee's slide\b0\par\par\uc2 659 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2011\par 11:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI, Nov 23 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee slid to an all-time low of 52.73 against the U.S. dollar on Tuesday but bounced back more than 1 percent on Wednesday after suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The rupee is under pressure as foreign investors are paring their exposure to Asia's third-largest economy amid global uncertainty and mounting worries over the domestic economy.\par \par \uc2 For more news on the rupee, see.\par \par \uc2 Foreign funds sold more than $500 million worth of Indian-listed shares over the five trading sessions to Monday, reducing net inflows for 2011 to under $300 million, a tiny sum compared with the record investments of more than $29 billion in 2010.\par \par \uc2 The rupee has lost more than 14 percent of its value this year, making it the worst performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia. The second-worst is the Thai baht, which has dropped only 3.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Views of investors on the rupee are now at their most pessimistic in more than three years, a Reuters poll showed on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 With the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) \uc2 seen reluctant to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to check the rupee's slide, some analysts are forecasting that the partially convertible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will slip to as low as 55 against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The following are some of the domestic macro-economic factors that are pushing the Indian unit down:\par \par \uc2 SLOWING ECONOMIC GROWTH\par \par \uc2 India's economy emerged largely unscathed from the 2008 financial crisis and looked set to achieve its pre-crisis growth rate of 9 percent, attracting investments from foreign players chasing higher returns.\par \par \uc2 However, a policy paralysis in the wake of a slew of graft scandals, combined with high inflation and rising interest rates, soon began to undermine the domestic demand-driven growth story.\par \par \uc2 The annual growth rate for the current fiscal year was originally budgeted at 9 percent but private economists have said that growth could be well below the 8 percent mark.\par \par \uc2 The Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already downgraded its growth projection for the fiscal year ending March 2012, from an original 8 percent to 7.6 percent now. Some policymakers have begun to concede that growth in the current fiscal year could slip as low as 7 percent.\par \par \uc2 WORSENING GOVERNMENT FINANCES\par \par \uc2 In February, New Delhi had budgeted a fiscal deficit, for the year ending March 2012, of 4.6 percent of gross domestic product on assumptions of high economic growth and low expenditure. However, a slowing economy and high subsidy spending are expected to upset those calculations, and many private economists are now predicting a full-year deficit of more than 5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Montek Singh Ahluwalia, the deputy head of the Planning Commission, last week conceded that meeting the budgeted fiscal deficit target would be a challenge and it was "not impossible" that the full-year deficit could swell to 5.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 In a global environment where investors are quick to punish fiscal slippages, the prospect of New Delhi missing its budgeted target by a full percentage point has heightened the investment risks for India.\par \par \uc2 WIDENING TRADE DEFICIT\par \par \uc2 The external balance of any economy is seen as the biggest driver of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Historically, India has been a current account deficit economy as it has been running a trade deficit.\par \par \uc2 However, it has been managing to fund its current account gap with foreign capital inflows, swamping equity markets betting on rosy prospects for economic growth. With that growth story now taking a beating, India now appears to be a relatively less attractive market for foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 In October, India had a trade deficit of $19.6 billion, its biggest in four years. the current account gap in the current fiscal year is expected to widen to 3 percent of GDP from 2.6 percent a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Rajesh Kumar Singh; Editing by John Chalmers and Richard Borsuk)\par \par \uc2 INDIA-RUPEE/FACTBOX   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111123e7bn000ge\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
