\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4-Indian rupee vulnerable after hitting record low\b0\par\par\uc2 940 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2011\par 04:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Rupee falls 0.3 pct; hits 52.73 vs dollar, further weakness seen\par \par \uc2 * Swelling current account deficit weighs\par \par \uc2 * Only cbank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to prevent slide - traders\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slumped 16.8 pct since late July high\par \par \uc2 (Updates with closing levels, adds comments)\par \par \uc2 By Swati Bhat\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Nov 22 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee fell to an all-time low on Tuesday as oil refiners and other companies scrambled to buy dollars, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  looking increasingly vulnerable to a swelling current account deficit and fears over the global economy and euro zone.\par \par \uc2 Exposure to short-term portfolio flows, a rising oil import bill and worsening government finances have heightened the risk for the rupee, Asia's worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, and the outlook remains bearish.\par \par \uc2 The rupee has skidded nearly 17 percent from a 2011 high reached in late July as risk-averse investors flee emerging markets, increasing the difficulties for a government already struggling with high inflation, slowing economic growth and a widening trade gap.\par \par \uc2 The failure of a U.S. super committee to reach a deal on debt restructuring could trigger another major round of selling of emerging market and risky assets.\par \par \uc2 The rupee closed 0.3 percent lower at 52.2950/3050 per dollar, after touching an all-time low of 52.73.\par \par \uc2 "At this point in time, the options for the RBI and the government are really limited. The movement in the rupee is dictated more by macro fundamentals rather than speculation," said N. Bhanumurthy, economist with National Institute of Public Finance and Policy in New Delhi.\par \par \uc2 "I will not be surprised if the rupee breaches the 53 level and stays there for some time. I don't see a pullback till external macro conditions change."\par \par \uc2 The falling rupee, combined with slowing growth, has added to a sense of urgency for a scandal-tainted government to push reforms to encourage investment.\par \par \uc2 The government has already raised ownership limits on government and corporate bonds and is considering allowing international retail investors direct access to Indian stocks , which have slumped about 22 percent so far this year.\par \par \uc2 Foreign funds have sold more than $500 million worth of shares over the last five trading sessions till Monday, reducing the net inflows in 2011 to under $300 million, sharply below record inflows of more than $29 billion seen in 2010.\par \par \uc2 "India's external position has become increasingly vulnerable to global risk appetite. Further weakness cannot be ruled out," \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  said in a research note.\par \par \uc2 The rupee is down 14.5 percent on the year, with the closest loser among other Asian units being the Thai baht, which has shed just 3.2 percent, followed by the Malaysian ringgit that is down 3 percent.\par \par \uc2 IS THE BOTTOM FALLING OUT?\par \par \uc2 The three-month offshore non-deliverable forward contracts\par \par \uc2 were quoted at 53.26, sharply below the onshore spot rate, suggesting a depreciation of another 1.8 percent from current levels.\par \par \uc2 One NDF trader said the breach of the previous record low of 52.20 low had caused nervousness and people were seeing this as a vicious circle with everyone -- including oil importers -- jumping in.\par \par \uc2 Oil accounts for about a third of India's total imports.\par \par \uc2 India's current account deficit is expected to be around 3 percent of gross domestic product (GDP) in the current fiscal year, compared with 2.7 percent a year ago.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI)\uc2 , which usually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb excess volatility, has been reluctant to step in and support the beleaguered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , adding to a sense that the falling rupee may be a one-way bet.\par \par \uc2 RBI Governor Duvvuri Subbarao said on Tuesday he could not comment on whether the bank was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem the rupee's slide, but it was watching the situation and would ensure the exchange rate does not impair economic stability.\par \par \uc2 "We expect that a reverse adjustment (in the rupee) will take place when the European situation resolves itself," Subbarao said.\par \par \uc2 Subir Gokarn, a deputy governor at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , had said last week the RBI would be careful about using \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves aggressively to protect the rupee's depreciation..\par \par \uc2 That view was echoed by Finance Minister Pranab Mukherjee on Tuesday, who blamed the fall on the international market and said that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have a limited effect.\par \par \uc2 "We expect there will be a self correction in the market," Mukherjee told reporters, without elaborating.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of selling dollars at around 51.79 on Monday, but the selling pressure on the rupee was too strong, traders said.\par \par \uc2 "The RBI's efforts to cut the excessive one-way move have been futile," said J. Moses Harding, head of the asset liability committee, and market and economic research at \uc2 IndusInd Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "There is not a single factor to bring the rupee bulls into the street who were in total control till July."\par \par \uc2 But some analysts echoed Mukherjee's view that the rupee could recover.\par \par \uc2 "I think INR has the potential to depreciate to 54 in the short term but in the meantime RBI may smooth the depreciation," said Sebastian Barbe, head of emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fixed income strategy at \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2 , in Paris.\par \par \uc2 "But given India's strong underlying story I'm confident that once the financial markets backdrop improves, the rupee can recover."\par \par \uc2 (Added reporting by Abhijit Neogy in NEW DELHI and Sujata Rao-Coverley in LONDON; Writing by Alistair Scrutton; Editing by John Chalmers, Ranjit Gangadharan and Aradhana Aravindan)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE/ (UPDATE 4)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111125e7bm0004m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
