\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 880 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2011\par 08:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: RATINGS FIRMS REACT TO LATEST US BUDGET FAILURE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The short statement came after a congressional committee tasked with slashing the U.S. budget gap failed to reach an agreement, a setback that triggers mandatory cuts to military spending and some social programs starting in 2013. (published 0114 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN FINANCE MINISTER WARY OF FOREIGN BOND-BUYING SCHEME TO WEAKEN YEN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's finance minister Tuesday shot down the idea of buying large amounts of foreign bonds to weaken the yen, saying it doesn't fit well with the nation's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy. (published 0440 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: WORLD BANK: GROWTH IN EAST ASIA TO MODERATE DUE TO WEAKER EXTERNAL DEMAND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economic growth in East Asia continues to moderate, mainly due to weakening external demand, underscoring the need for governments to focus on driving local demand and productivity amid uncertainties in Europe and a global growth slowdown, the World Bank said in a report. (published 0251 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WORLD BANK: CHINA SURPLUS WILL FALL IF REAL YUAN FURTHER APPRECIATES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's current account surplus may continue to decline, especially if the yuan appreciates further in real terms, World Bank China Economist Ardo Hansson said Tuesday. (published 0409 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: FORMER PBOC ADVISER: CONTROLLING INFLATION NO LONGER A POLICY PRIORITY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's inflation has peaked and controlling price growth is no longer a policy priority, a former adviser to the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Tuesday. (published 0401 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PBOC ADVISER: DIFFICULT FOR ECONOMY TO GROW AT DOUBLE-DIGIT RATE GOING FORWARD \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It will be difficult for China's economy to grow at a double-digit rate going forward as exports will face increased pressure next year due to weakening U.S. and European economies, an adviser to the People's Bank of China said Tuesday. (published 0706 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S SWAN SAYS GLOBAL ECON CONDITIONS VERY DIFFICULT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's government plans to issue its mid year budget review by the end of the calendar year and it will be framed against a backdrop of "very difficult global economic conditions," Treasurer Wayne Swan said Tuesday. (published 0445 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JPMORGAN SAYS AUSTRALIA'S RBA TO CUT RATES 25 BPS IN DECEMBER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will cut interest rates in the country to 4.25% from the current 4.50% when the bank meets in December, \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Tuesday. (published 0125 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA GOVT ECONOMIST: ECONOMIC GROWTH WILL SLOW TO 'MEDIUM' SPEED NEXT FEW YEARS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's economic growth will likely slow to "medium" speed--that is, growth of 6%-7% annually--over the next few years, with a slowdown in investment growth, a government economist said at a forum Tuesday. (published 0253 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WORLD BANK: CHINA COULD STILL SEE SOFT LANDING ON POLICY ADJUSTMENT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China faces rising risks to its economic growth from areas such as its weakening property market, but ample room for policy adjustment means Beijing should still be able to guide the economy toward a moderate slowdown, the World Bank said in a report Tuesday. (published 0200 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: PBOC, HKMA DOUBLE SIZE OF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -SWAP DEAL TO CNY400 BLN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China and Hong Kong said Tuesday they have doubled the value of a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swap to CNY400 billion as Beijing seeks to expand the pool of yuan traded offshore. (published 0438 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: S KOREA SHORT-TERM DEBT FALLS BY THE MOST SINCE 2008 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's short-term external debt declined in the third quarter by the most since the fourth quarter of 2008, during the global financial crisis, due to the repatriation of funds by foreign banks, while the government's efforts to reduce the risk of sudden rapid capital outflows are also taking effect. (published 0625 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: INDIAN RUPEE FALLS TO NEW ALL-TIME LOW AGAINST US DOLLAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Indian rupee tumbled to a new all-time low against the U.S. dollar Tuesday as importers continued to accumulate the greenback on fears that the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would weaken further in the absence of any aggressive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 0606 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INDIA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FINANCE MINISTER: RUPEE'S WEAKNESS DUE TO GLOBAL UNCERTAINTIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  has limited scope to check the rupee's decline against the dollar as the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's weakness stems from global uncertainties that are driving investors to the safe-haven greenback, the finance minister said Tuesday. (published 0629 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TAIWAN REGULATOR ASKS LOCAL BANKS TO RAISE LOAN-LOSS PROVISIONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Taiwan's financial regulator on Tuesday asked domestic banks to increase their loan-loss provisions to strengthen the island's banking system, as the worsening global economic outlook is increasing the risk of loan defaults. (published 0218 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ASIA: NEW JAPAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  STRATEGY LEAVES MARKETS UNCERTAIN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's new tactics for trying to weaken the yen, mixing highly public forays into the market with stealth \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , has left market players uncertain over what may come next, which appears to be just what the authorities want. (published 0550 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: SINGAPORE OCTOBER CPI LIKELY ROSE 5.2% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore's consumer price inflation likely edged lower in October, as previous rounds of tightening by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and a slowing global economy begun to bite. (published 0527 GMT) [ 22-11-11 0707GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111122e7bm000bi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
