\par\par \b \uc2 India Economy Secretary: Rupee Can't Slide Beyond A Point\b0\par\par\uc2 132 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 November 2011\par 09:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has limited ability to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to check the rupee's sharp depreciation, a senior government official said Monday.\par \par \uc2 "The rupee cannot slide beyond a point," Economic Affairs Secretary R. Gopalan told reporters. "The ability to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also limited," he added.\par \par \uc2 The rupee has weakened about 15% against the U.S. dollar since April, hurt by continued capital outflows in the wake of the global economic troubles that have made investors averse to risk.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was last trading at INR51.78, its highest in 32 months.\par \par \uc2 -By Prasanta Sahu and Mukesh Jagota, Dow Jones Newswires; +91-11-43563311; anant.kala@dowjones.com [ 21-11-11 0824GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111121e7bl000g0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
