\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Rupee Near Record Low On Weak Stocks, Euro; Bonds Tad Up\b0\par\par\uc2 441 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 November 2011\par 13:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par                    As Of 1200 GMT \par                          Latest      Change \par    USD/INR               52.16       +0.82 \par    8.79% 2021 Bond       8.83%       Unchanged \par    Call Rate             8.73%       +8BPS \par    Forward Dollar Premium/Discount* \par    Oct                   1.3050      -0.2650 \par    *Forward Dollar Premium/Discount are midpoints of bid-offer spreads \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee fell for a sixth session Monday to reach within a striking distance of its all-time low against the U.S. dollar, tracking weak local stocks and due to concerns over the euro-zone debt crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dollar buying by local firms to settle month-end bills also pressured the rupee. The dollar was at INR52.16, up from INR51.34 late Friday and near its record high of INR52.1950 on March 3, 2009.\par \par \uc2 "If the current factors continue, the rupee is likely to be under pressure. The global situation is very uncertain and one has look for clearer signs from the euro zone," said Sudarshan Bhatt, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at \uc2 Corporation Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The euro and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tied to investor confidence came under pressure Monday as growing concerns about the global economic outlook and heightened anxiety over deadlocked U.S. debt talks rattled traders.\par \par \uc2 The rupee, which keenly tracks the euro, has been Asia's worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and has weakened nearly 17% against the U.S. dollar this fiscal year that started April 1.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bombay Stock Exchange\uc2 's Sensitive Index slumped 2.6% to 15,946.10.\par \par \uc2 Also, Economic Affairs Secretary R. Gopalan told reporters Monday that \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has limited ability to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to check the rupee's sharp depreciation.\par \par \uc2 In the sovereign bond markets, government bond prices ended a tad higher, ahead of a INR100 billion bond buyback later this week. Tight liquidity conditions limited the upside.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark 8.79% 2021 bond ended at INR99.76, from Friday's INR99.73.\par \par \uc2 "Liquidity is not going to improve significantly unless there are a slew of bond buybacks for a couple of weeks," said a dealer at a state-run bank.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will sell INR80 billion worth of treasury bills, INR58.6 billion of state development loans and INR130 billion of government bonds this week.\par \par \uc2 Indian banks borrowed INR1.27 trillion at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's liquidity facility earlier Monday and have been borrowing INR800 billion-INR1.3 trillion daily for the last two weeks, underscoring tight liquidity conditions.\par \par \uc2 -By Sourav Mishra and Sudeep Jain, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 6145 6112; sudeep.jain@dowjones.com [ 21-11-11 1242GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111121e7bl000t3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
