\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  cbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervened \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  mkt in Sept after 9 mth hiatus\b0\par\par\uc2 292 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2011\par 15:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Nov 11 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in September, after following a hands-off approach for nine straight months, as the unit fell to its lowest level in more than two years, its monthly bulletin showed on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The rupee had moved in a wide band of 45.79 per dollar to 49.90 in September. It had also dropped 8.8 percent between July and September, its worst quarterly fall in three years.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India (RBI) \uc2 has always maintained that it does not target any specific exchange rate on the rupee and only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent excessive volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Reuters had reported in mid-September that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely sold dollars to prevent the rupee from falling sharply.\par \par \uc2 RBI sold $845 million in September, while it did not buy any dollars, in its first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since November 2010, when it had bought $1.37 billion and sold $500 million, the bulletin showed.\par \par \uc2 On a net basis, November 2010 was the RBI's biggest monthly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since June 2009, when it had bought $1.04 billion.\par \par \uc2 In 2010, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought a net $1.85 billion, compared with net sales of $5.8 billion in 2009 when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the rupee from depreciating sharply.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the partially convertible rupee closed at 50.1150/1250, 0.1 percent stronger than its Wednesday close of 50.1750/1850. Earlier in the day, it had dropped to 50.4200 against the dollar, a level last seen on April 28, 2009.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Swati Bhat; Editing by Aradhana Aravindan)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INDIA\b0 \highlight\uc2 -CBANK-\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 /   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111111e7bb001k0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
