\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ IMF Executive Director: Would Be Foolish To Assign "Right Value" To Indian Rupee\b0\par\par\uc2 611 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2011\par 09:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 3 Oct 2011 03:27 EDT *DJ  IMF Executive: Fully Support RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Management Policy\par \par \uc2 3 Oct 2011 03:27 EDT *DJ  IMF Executive: Essential To Steadily Shrink India's Fiscal Deficit\par \par \uc2 3 Oct 2011 03:28 EDT *DJ  IMF Executive: Low Fiscal Gap Crucial For India To Sustain High Long-Term Growth\par \par \uc2 3 Oct 2011 03:28 EDT *DJ  IMF Executive: India Can Absorb External Shocks If It Pushes Economic Reforms\par \par \uc2 3 Oct 2011 03:28 EDT *DJ  IMF Executive: Muted FY Growth Not Critical To Medium-Term Trend\par \par \uc2 3 Oct 2011 03:28 EDT *DJ  IMF Executive: Monetary Policy To Note Demand Pressures From High Fiscal Gap\par \par \uc2 3 Oct 2011 03:29 EDT DJ  INTERVIEW: IMF Executive: Can't Assign Specific Value To Indian Rupee\par \par \uc2 -- IMF executive director in full agreement with RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  management policy\par \par \uc2 -- Would be foolhardy to assign specific value to Indian rupee\par \par \uc2 -- India needs to steadily shrink fiscal gap to sustain long-term growth\par \par \uc2 By Abhrajit Gangopadhyay\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI (Dow Jones)-- The Indian rupee can't be pegged to a definite value against the U.S. dollar and its recent weakness doesn't signal any fundamental economic shift, but mirrors a global strengthening of the greenback, an executive director at the International Monetary Fund said.\par \par \uc2 "It would be foolhardy to say that such and such is the right value of the rupee, no matter what is happening in the globe and in the Indian economy," Arvind Virmani told Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 The partly convertible Indian rupee has weakened nearly 10% against the U.S. dollar since the start of the fiscal year on April 1 -- one of worst performing Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- but the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  much to stem the local unit's steady slide.\par \par \uc2 "I am fully in agreement with the declared RBI policy to only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to dampen excessive volatility in the rupee-dollar exchange rate, while leaving it free to adjust to any changes in its underlying trend or cycles due to basic changes in economic factors," Virmani said.\par \par \uc2 A weak rupee adds to the prolonged inflationary pressure in Asia's third-largest economy, which imports nearly 80% of its oil requirements and pays mostly in U.S. dollars.\par \par \uc2 Inflation has remained the top concern for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has raised the key policy rate a dozen times over past 18 months. Economists largely blame supply bottlenecks for driving India's stubborn inflation, and high global commodity prices offer little hope of price pressures easing sharply anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 "Monetary policy also has to take account of demand-side pressures arising from fiscal deficit," Virmani said.\par \par \uc2 India aims to limit its fiscal deficit to 4.6% of gross domestic product this fiscal year, but analysts widely expect the government to miss the target as revenue growth stutters amid a slowing economy and as subsidy payouts mount.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the government raised its full-year borrowing target by INR528 billion, but maintained it would stick to the budget gap aim -- a claim most analysts don't agree with.\par \par \uc2 "It is essential to put the fiscal deficit on clear and unambiguous downward trajectory if India wants to sustain growth in the 8.5%-9% range for the next two decades," Virmani said.\par \par \uc2 India's economy is estimated to grow at 8.2% this fiscal year, slowing from an 8.5% expansion last year.\par \par \uc2 -By Abhrajit Gangopadhyay, Dow Jones Newswires; 91-11-43563301 abhrajit.gangopadhyay@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 03, 2011 03:29 ET (07:29 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020111003e7a30017s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
