\par\par \b \uc2 DJ HEARD ON THE STREET: India Struggles With Sinking Rupee\b0\par\par\uc2 By Harsh Joshi  \par \uc2 A DOW JONES COLUMN  \par \uc2 493 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2011\par 15:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 As if New Delhi didn't have enough on its plate, it now has the slumping rupee to contend with.\par \par \uc2 The Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lagged its Asian peers this fiscal year that began in April, with a 10% fall against the dollar. The country's current account and fiscal deficits, coupled with the risk of further market turmoil, could see it go lower still.\par \par \uc2 But unlike other Asian countries, whose vibrant exporters benefit from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness, India is a net importer. A weakening rupee risks driving already high inflation up further.\par \par \uc2 This rapid decline in the rupee also highlights problems with India's balance of payments. Its current account deficit is usually masked by capital inflows, thanks to investors seeking emerging-market exposure. But such hot money can flow out when investors flee to safer assets. Since August, investors have sold off net $2.6 billion of Indian shares, according to data from the capital markets regulator.\par \par \uc2 Companies aren't safe, either. Oil companies, for one, are already hinting their earnings will be hit due to the rupee's decline. India imports nearly 80% of its crude-oil requirements and government-owned refiners are partly compensated by New Delhi for selling most fuels at subsidized rates. But the extent and timing of such compensation is uncertain. And given India's weak fiscal position and the government's need to spend on social schemes, such aid anyway won't be enough to make the oil companies profitable.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintains that it will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market only to curb excessive volatility--a measure the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not defined. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reportedly sold dollars recently to pull the rupee back from a 28-month low. But such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to become a frequent phenomenon and won't be enough to reverse the downward trend.\par \par \uc2 Not all stand to lose. India's software exporters, which earn nearly 90% of their revenues overseas, are optimistic about this year. Yet overall, India is one country that could do without more rapid falls in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Harsh Joshi is a Heard on the Street columnist in Mumbai. He has been with Dow Jones Newswires since 2007 and covered the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , money and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, and the banking sector. He can be reached at harsh.joshi@dowjones.com or at +91-22-6145-6101. )\par \par \uc2 (TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 03, 2011 09:17 ET (13:17 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020111003e7a3003ja\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
