\par\par \b \uc2 HEARD ON THE STREET: Daily Digest\b0\par\par\uc2 3,586 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2011\par 21:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 HEARD ON THE STREET: No Easy Fix In Leveraging EFSF\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Richard Barley \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The shelf-life of Europe's bailout plans is getting shorter. European parliaments have nearly finished ratifying the expansion of the euro zone's EUR440 billion bailout fund. But already the debate has shifted to whether the European Financial Stability Facility needs to be expanded again given the escalation of the crisis. There are no simple fixes.\par \par \uc2 The fear is the EFSF isn't big enough to halt contagion, particularly if Greece defaults. Loans to Ireland, Portugal and now Greece add up to EUR120 billion. A bigger bailout for Greece and loans to help some euro-zone countries recapitalize their banks could leave just EUR250 billion to fight contagion by buying bonds. The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has bought around EUR85 billion of bonds since mid-August alone but is expected to stop buying when the EFSF is ratified.\par \par \uc2 Germany, among others, opposes increasing the size of the EFSF. Turning the EFSF into a bank that could borrow to boost its firepower, as suggested by U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner, also seems a non-starter. The private market can't provide cheap enough funding while the ECB is clear this lending lies outside its mandate. Besides, it could threaten the triple-A rating of the facility and its key sovereign guarantors.\par \par \uc2 A better option might be to leverage the EFSF by turning it into an insurer. For example, if the EFSF was to insure the first 20% of losses on government bonds, its remaining EUR250 billion of firepower could be increased to EUR1.25 trillion. That might ease fears about Spanish and Italian near-term issuance. But this isn't straightforward either. It might lead to the creation of a two-tier bond market, making investing more complex and deterring buyers. RBS reckons savings might be relatively small, reducing 10-year Italian yields by 0.6 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 What if the EFSF insured the ECB's bond purchases, potentially allowing it to buy much more debt? The ECB's bond-buying program is deeply controversial already and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is sure to resist any pressure to extend what it claims is a temporary measure to repair the monetary transmission mechanism, not an exercise to promote financial stability. And it may simply give investors an opportunity to exit the market.\par \par \uc2 Ideally, the EFSF might be used to guarantee bank debt, given the risk of a credit crunch in the euro zone while bank funding markets remain closed. That might break the loop between sovereign and bank woes. But aid for troubled states is politically difficult enough; cross-border aid for banks may be even more troublesome.\par \par \uc2 Germany and other creditor countries may be reluctant to do anything that eases the pressure on governments to restructure their economies and tackle deficits. But if Europe can't find a way to increase the EFSF's firepower, the pressure on the euro-zone financial system might become overwhelming.\par \par \uc2 HEARD ON THE STREET: Japan's Brief Bounce Back\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By James Simms \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The mood among Japan's corporations brightened last quarter, but there is plenty about the country's economy that should be a cause for worry.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan's key tankan survey Monday showed that confidence among manufacturers large and small is returning to levels last seen before March's earthquake. Auto makers and electronics companies were among the most optimistic, as supply chains came back online and temporary factors bolstered sales of televisions and other electronics.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After a plunge in sentiment in the June survey, however, a rebound was always likely. Looking ahead, the data appear less rosy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Now, the nation's exporters, in particular, will have to contend with the looming global slowdown and the yen's record strength.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The tankan survey showed manufacturers are already worried that overseas demand is falling short of supply, a trend that's forecast to worsen in the next quarter as large manufacturers reported a sharp jump in their excess inventories.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The survey also suggests some of Japan's businesses may be underestimating their problems. Large manufacturers still have the basis of their earnings forecasts pegged at Y81.15 to the dollar for this year, compared with the current rate of Y77.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Beyond the tankan, the data are a bit gloomy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Markit/JMMA Purchasing Managers' Index survey for September, released Friday, showed new export orders at Japanese companies fell at the fastest pace since April. The report blamed the strength of the yen and slowing Chinese economic growth lift. And while the sales of energy-saving appliances rose amid a power-shortage scare and more consumers bought television sets following a switch to digital broadcasting in July, the effects of these trends have already played out.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Government data also released Friday points to a 2.5% drop in industrial production in September, led by sectors that make flat-panel televisions, refrigerators and semiconductors, the government said Friday. Output is projected to rise 3.8% in October helped by a 13.6% jump in transportation equipment after a sharp slowdown in production after the quake. But a number of recent government production forecasts, based on company surveys, have been far off the mark. Other recent data points have indicated that greater numbers of people have given up the search for work and a weakening trade surplus.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Though sentiment among big business has lifted off recent lows, for Japan there's likely to be more bad news to come.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HEARD ON THE STREET: UK Deserves Credit For Easing\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Simon Nixon \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 George Osborne's announcement that the U.K. Treasury is exploring options for credit easing is potentially very significant. It has been clear for some time that the U.K. is at serious risk of a new credit crunch as the closure of European bank funding markets and the global economic slowdown is forcing banks to accelerate deleveraging plans. The Chancellor of the Exchequer's commitment to use taxpayer money to boost the flow of credit to small and medium sized companies is therefore welcome.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Of course, much depends on how the policy is structured. So far, the Chancellor of the Exchequer has provided few details. One option is for the Treasury to buy corporate bonds, much as the Bank of England did in 2009 under its Asset Purchase Facility, in an attempt to open up the U.K. corporate bond market. This policy could be expanded to include securitization bonds backed by bank loans to small and medium-sized businesses loans into bonds which could then be acquired by the Treasury or a Treasury-funded vehicle, freeing up bank liquidity for reinvestment in new loans.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the U.K. corporate bond market is relatively small and tends to be used by bigger companies that have less trouble getting access to funding. Meanwhile, a policy designed to create a new market for SME securitizations could take time to become effective and could be complex to administer. A bolder - but far more controversial policy - would be for the Treasury to target the monetary policy transmission mechanism directly, whether by buying bank bonds or re-introducing long-term funding facilities. Banks would be far more likely to lend if they were sure of plentiful cheap funding.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But whatever form of credit easing is adopted, the real significance of the Chancellor's move is that it removes a political logjam among U.K. policymakers: Mr. Osborne has explicitly recognized that only the elected government has a mandate to expose taxpayers to potential losses and that politicians cannot offload their responsibilities onto central banks. Euro-zone governments should take note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HEARD ON THE STREET: India Struggles With Sinking Rupee\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Harsh Joshi \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As if New Delhi didn't have enough on its plate, it now has the slumping rupee to contend with.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Indian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has lagged its Asian peers this fiscal year that began in April, with a 10% fall against the dollar. The country's current account and fiscal deficits, coupled with the risk of further market turmoil, could see it go lower still.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But unlike other Asian countries, whose vibrant exporters benefit from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakness, India is a net importer. A weakening rupee risks driving already high inflation up further.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This rapid decline in the rupee also highlights problems with India's balance of payments. Its current account deficit is usually masked by capital inflows, thanks to investors seeking emerging-market exposure. But such hot money can flow out when investors flee to safer assets. Since August, investors have sold off net $2.6 billion of Indian shares, according to data from the capital markets regulator.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Companies aren't safe, either. Oil companies, for one, are already hinting their earnings will be hit due to the rupee's decline. India imports nearly 80% of its crude-oil requirements and government-owned refiners are partly compensated by New Delhi for selling most fuels at subsidized rates. But the extent and timing of such compensation is uncertain. And given India's weak fiscal position and the government's need to spend on social schemes, such aid anyway won't be enough to make the oil companies profitable.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  maintains that it will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market only to curb excessive volatility a measure the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has not defined. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reportedly sold dollars recently to pull the rupee back from a 28-month low. But such \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is unlikely to become a frequent phenomenon and won't be enough to reverse the downward trend.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Not all stand to lose. India's software exporters, which earn nearly 90% of their revenues overseas, are optimistic about this year. Yet overall, India is one country that could do without more rapid falls in its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HEARD ON THE STREET: Ghosts Could Be Lurking In Banking Machines\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By David Reilly \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When it comes to banks, what you don't know can definitely hurt. [ 10-03-11 1506ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That's a hard-learned crisis lesson and helps explain, in part, why big U.S. banks have taken such a pounding of late. As Europe wobbles, investors in firms like \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan Chase\f28 \uc2 \f2  (JPM), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  (C), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  (GS), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BAC) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  (MS), are again trying desperately to figure out their euro-zone risks. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2 , in particular, suffered for this reason Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A big problem for investors is varied, sparse or confusing disclosure about derivatives exposures even after the financial crisis showed that the inter-connectedness of firms is often as big a threat as their size. Indeed, in just the U.S., the five big banks account for 96%n of $332 trillion in face value, or notional, derivatives held by leading firms.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Granted, executives offered some detail on European exposures when reporting second-quarter results, saying they were manageable. But investors were often given net, not gross, exposures, and can't tell how exposed the banks' massive derivatives portfolios may be to big European banks through counterparty risk.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even bankers acknowledge shortcomings. In a recent speech, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  CEO \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vikram Pandit\f28 \uc2 \f2  noted derivatives "remain too opaque." He added, "Lack of transparency inhibits market discipline, obscures risk and leaves nasty surprises buried in the system."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If only Citi walked Mr. Pandit's talk. During the bank's second-quarter call, analyst Mike Mayo of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Agricole\f28 \uc2 \f2  asked about the bank's exposure to troubled European countries. "What is the gross number and what's the difference between the gross and the net?" Citi CFO \f28 \uc2 \f2 John Gerspach\f28 \uc2 \f2  replied: "I don't think that the gross number is relevant."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But it is. As \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  found Friday, confusion over gross and net exposures can spook investors. The difference is usually due to cash and collateral held against loans as well as derivatives used as hedges. Yet investors often don't know how big the hedges are an important point since hedges often aren't perfect or who the counterparty is.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Divining counterparty risk, and concentrations of it, is tough, and disclosures vary. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  gives a country breakdown of derivatives exposures. \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  doesn't do that, but does give a derivatives breakdown based on region. In its second-quarter filing \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  said it had net derivatives assets with exposure to Europe, the Middle East and Africa of $35 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But there is no similar geographic breakdown for the firm's gross derivatives assets of nearly $1.4 trillion. If a counterparty fails, gross figures may prove more important for investors. "The asset can disappear and you're left with the liability," explains \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  accounting analyst David Zion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Plus, during periods of extreme market turmoil a problem with a counterparty's counterparty something almost impossible to measure can come to haunt even banks that have chosen their trading partners well.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Given the difficulty in gauging which banks hold what risks, investors are often choosing to simply sell bank stocks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That leaves banks with a choice as third-quarter reporting season approaches: provide more and better information about derivatives and European exposures, or continue to get tarred with the euro-fear brush.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HEARD ON THE STREET: Is H-P In \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Future?\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Rolfe Winkler \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hewlett-Packard Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (HPQ) might as well be a ball of yarn for all the fun \f28 \uc2 \f2 Larry Ellison\f28 \uc2 \f2  is having batting it around. Is he softening up his rival for a career-capping takeover?\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oracle Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (ORCL) said this week that it passed when given an opportunity in April to buy software-maker \f28 \uc2 \f2 Autonomy Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (AU.LN, AUTNY), saying that the company's market value of $6 billion was "already extremely overpriced." The implication was that H-P was foolish for paying $10.3 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Autonomy shot back that it never shopped itself to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2 . In turn, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2  released slides it received from Autonomy's investment banker, Frank Quattrone, that suggest it was indeed being shopped.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Whatever the truth, it's just \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2 's latest broadside at H-P. In March, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2  said it would stop developing certain software that runs on a line of H-P servers. Last year Ellison, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2 's chief executive, slammed the H-P board for firing CEO \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mark Hurd\f28 \uc2 \f2 , then promptly hired him.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All in good fun, perhaps. But could it serve a larger purpose? For starters, it's easier for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2 's hardware unit to win H-P customers if H-P looks bad. More intriguing, after more than halving since February, H-P shares fetch just six times forward earnings. With its board of directors seemingly in disarray, the company looks vulnerable. And some have started to speculate about a bid from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It would be a big deal: Even before a premium, H-P's market capitalization is $48 billion. But \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2  has $17 billion of net cash and ample capacity to borrow. Its own shares, at 15 times forward earnings, look an attractive acquisition \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . And \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2  has two aces up its sleeve. President Safra Catz has led the integration of multiple acquisitions, squeezing great returns for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2  shareholders. And its president, Hurd, knows H-P better than anyone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2  could nab H-P on the cheap it might extract impressive returns. And Ellison might cap his career by creating an integrated software, hardware and services giant rivaling \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Business Machines Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (IBM).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But dig into the strategic benefits, and the rationale for a deal becomes murkier. For starters, H-P's PC and printer businesses 49% of revenue and 44% of operating profit last quarter don't obviously fit with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Both could be sold or spun off, although PCs would pose more of a challenge than cash-cow printers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Adding H-P's enterprise hardware and services businesses would make more sense, helping turn \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2  into a one-stop shop for information technology. But even those aren't a perfect fit. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2  prefers to sell products based on its own intellectual property, argued analyst Mark Moerdler of Sanford C. Bernstein in a research note. Much of H-P's enterprise hardware is based on other's technology. Meanwhile, H-P's services business does mostly low-end support work, not higher-end consulting typical of IBM.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 H-P's smaller software business seems a good fit including Autonomy, a software specialist in an area where \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2  is weak. Another risk for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2  shareholders: Through H-P, they would be getting into lower-margin businesses than their existing software franchise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2 's growth in enterprise software is threatened by cheaper "cloud-based" software from the likes of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Salesforce.com Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (CRM) and Workday. And in the past, its aggressive deals have worked out well. Despite the obvious execution risks, further falls in H-P's share price might make it too cheap for Ellison to resist.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HEARD ON THE STREET: Morgan Finds It Tough To Stand Tall\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    By David Reilly \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During the financial crisis, bankers at relatively strong institutions would joke they were the tallest midget in the bunch. The problem for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  is that it is still among the shortest.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While in far better financial shape than in 2008, Morgan remains the smallest and to some extent most vulnerable of the big, U.S. financial institutions. It also has a tendency for trading stumbles, even if it bucked that trend in the second quarter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This goes part of the way in explaining why its stock and credit-default swap spreads have been getting hit far harder than peers on fears related to Europe, the slowing global economy and a trading downturn. And it may also be why Morgan seems more susceptible to market chatter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Friday, Morgan's shares tumbled about 7% and its CDS spreads continued to widen due in part to confusion over its exposure to Europe. A report pegged its "net" exposure to French banks at $39 billion, although this was actually a "gross" figure. On a net basis, which takes into account collateral and hedges, its exposure is zero, according to a person familiar with the matter. And the $39 billion figure is for the end of 2010; the latest gross figure is $21.6 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That confusion aside, there are deeper reasons for Morgan's travails. Investors remain unconvinced its Merrillesque strategy of running both a trading \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and a big brokerage will bear fruit. Most importantly, investors aren't sure the old Wall Street trading model will be able to generate sufficient returns in the face of more-stringent regulations and subdued trading. And there seems to be little chance someone will suddenly come along and bid for Morgan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 So although much of the current fear looks overdone, and Morgan's about 50% discount to tangible book value seems unwarranted, the storm clouds aren't likely to part anytime soon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HEARD ON THE STREET: Red-Hot Metals Could Burn LME's Suitors\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Hester Plumridge \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Independent exchanges are as hot as molten steel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After 130 years fending off takeover interest, the member-owned London Metal Exchange has received a preliminary offer so attractive it's considering a sale, and has opened up the field to other bidders. More than 10 parties are considering bids, with a deal likely to value the group at up to $1.6 billion, according to a person familiar with the situation. But LME's suitors should be careful not to get their fingers burned.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Exchanges globally are consolidating as a result of low growth in mature markets and the loss of market share to new entrants in areas such as cash equities. The LME, which dominates global trading in metal futures, offers rare growth. Market volatility is driving increased hedging. Chinese growth is fueling demand for metals like copper. LME's trading volumes are up 20% annually. Earnings should hit $27 million in 2012, from $15 million last year, forecasts \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2 . [ 10-03-11 1506ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the $1.6 billion price tag values LME at almost 60 times forward earnings, versus the 13 times that peer \f28 \uc2 \f2 CME Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  trades. To justify that price, would-be buyers expected to include a joint bid from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the Singapore Exchange, as well as interest from CME and IntercontinentalExchange need to be confident of two things: synergies from clearing LME's trades, and a massive rise in the fees it charges.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But that's a risky bet. Clearing synergies would likely be around $26 million a year for groups such as CME and ICE, noted \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Taxed and capitalized, that's worth only around $180 million. Meanwhile, the impact of raising fees is harder to judge. LME currently charges around 25 cents per trade; other exchanges charge up to $1.00. But higher fees might dent trading volumes. LME's members, which pay the fees, might also seek a price cap or other concessions in any deal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LME's members are happy with the current ownership structure, according to people familiar with their thinking, and it will take a high price to secure the 75% approval required for a deal from those members that own its shares. But that is no reason for bidders to lose their heads. LME is a rare commodity, not a gold mine. [ 10-03-11 1506ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4257   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020111003e7a30003n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
