\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-INTERVIEW-UPDATE 2-India has no plans to curb capital outflows\b0\par\par\uc2 By Manoj Kumar   \par \uc2 770 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2011\par 04:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats story issued late on Monday)\par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI, Sept 26 (Reuters) - India has no plans to tax or curb capital outflows, a top finance ministry adviser said on Monday, adding the economy could still grow at around 8 percent in the current fiscal year to end-March despite increased global economic uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 Foreign institutional investors (FIIs) have sold more than $2.2 billion worth of Indian shares since August, as fears of a recession in the developed world lowered risk appetite. The main 30-share benchmark BSE index is down nearly 22 percent so far this year.\par \par \uc2 The partially convertible rupee , which hit a 28-month low last Friday at 49.90 per dollar, has lost over 11 percent from its 2011 high hit in late July and remains the worst performer among major Asian peers.\par \par \uc2 "I do not think we have anything to suggest to us any such thing," Dipak Dasgupta, principal economic adviser to the \b \highlight22\uc2 ministry of finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , told Reuters when asked whether India would consider imposing curbs on capital outflows.\par \par \uc2 "Instead of trying to restrict the outflow, it is quite the opposite. In fact, what we are trying to do, as you have seen in the ECB (external commercial borrowing) regulation changes, is actually to make it a little more comfortable yet prudent to get inflows of capital to come to India."\par \par \uc2 India relaxed its overseas borrowing rules on Sept. 15, raising the borrowing limit for companies and allowing firms to raise Chinese yuan-denominated debt in an attempt to woo capital inflows amid heightened global uncertainty. [ID: nL3E7KG04A]\par \par \uc2 FALLING RUPEE\par \par \uc2 Many analysts have called for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent the recent sharp slide in the rupee from fuelling imported inflation, which could further stoke domestic price pressures.\par \par \uc2 "What drives the dollar's valuation is so far bigger. Whatever we may try to do, absolutely you cannot affect it," Dasgupta said.\par \par \uc2 Last week, a deputy governor of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) \uc2 would maintain its stance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market only to reduce volatility.\par \par \uc2 The RBI had refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for eight consecutive months until July, latest \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 Analysts have warned the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should not utilise its limited \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of around $316 billion to protect the economy from imported inflation.\par \par \uc2 "Our trade gap has widened considerably. Our dependence on short-term loans has increased considerably. So even if the RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the rupee may not recover substantially," said Rupa Rege Nitsure, chief economist at Bank of India.\par \par \uc2 "Companies have to factor in strong downward bias in rupee and accordingly hedge their positions," she added.\par \par \uc2 DOMESTIC DEMAND TAILWIND\par \par \uc2 Dasgupta said that while a global economic slowdown would have a knock-on effect on the Indian economy, strong domestic demand -- strengthened after normal monsoon rains -- could help Asia's third-largest economy log around 8 percent growth in the current fiscal year to end-March 2012.\par \par \uc2 "We are headed towards plus 8 percent growth in this fiscal and it is far too early to change that assumption," said Dasgupta, adding any decline in global crude and commodity prices would benefit the economy.\par \par \uc2 "We will probably recover in the last two quarters of this fiscal year."\par \par \uc2 India meets over 75 percent of its oil demand through imports. Brent futures for November <LCOc1> fell on Monday to $2.31 to a low of $101.66 a barrel. Brent is down 9 percent so far in September.\par \par \uc2 Indian economic growth could fall by around 0.4 percentage points in this fiscal year from earlier esmalntimates of around 8.5 percent, if the OECD (Organisation for Economic Co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Development) region slows by 2 percentage points, he said.\par \par \uc2 Dasgupta said he expects headline inflation to moderate in coming months, easing to 8 percent in December, and then slide further to 5-7 percent by March.\par \par \uc2 The RBI has raised interest rates 12 times in 18 months but inflation, which in August accelerated to 9.78 percent, remains at more than twice its comfort level. That puts the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the awkward position of fighting high prices amid mounting worries about the health of the global economy.\par \par \uc2 "Part of that (easing inflation) is the base effect, but it is not just the base effect. It is also due to good rainfall," Dasgupta said.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Malini Menon and Catherine Evans)\par \par \uc2 INDIA-ECONOMY/CAPITAL   (UPDATE 2) (REPEAT)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110930e79r00043\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
