\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ BLOG/MarketBeat: Plunging  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Complicate Inflation Fight\b0\par\par\uc2 666 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2011\par 10:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's Market Beat blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/marketbeat"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/marketbeat }}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 By Alex Frangos\par \par \uc2 Just because markets are crashing doesn't mean inflation is going away. In fact, the situation could be getting worse due to falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Singapore reported Friday that consumer prices rose 5.7% in the year ending August, faster than the 5.4% rate in July. The Singapore dollar meanwhile is down nearly 8% this month against the U.S. dollar. A weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes imported goods, especially commodities priced in dollars, more expensive, adding to inflationary pressures.\par \par \uc2 A similar scenario is playing out elsewhere. Inflation remains elevated in Brazil, India and South Korea, and now it will get a boost from plummeting \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Since the start of September, the greenback is up 19% against the Brazilian real, 18% against the South African rand and nearly 9% against the Korean won. It's up more than 7.5% against the Indian rupee.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, there has been some relief on the commodities front in the past couple of days, with copper and oil dropping. But these commodities are notoriously volatile and could rebound quickly.\par \par \uc2 And even if they don't rebound, inflation is bad enough in some places, such as India, that these countries will now miss out on what could have been a surprise inflation-relief valve.\par \par \uc2 As The Wall Street Journal's Shefali Anand reported Thursday:\par \par \uc2 A weakening rupee "raises the inflation almost automatically," says Abheek Barua, chief economist at \uc2 HDFC Bank Ltd\uc2 . Most commodities imported by India, particularly oil, are denominated in dollars, making these expensive for India.\par \par \uc2 "The recent fall in global commodity prices is now mostly offset by the depreciation in [the rupee] and thus is unlikely to provide the much-needed and anticipated relief on imported inflation front," writes Jay Shankar, economist at Religare India. He like, many commentators in \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is calling on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to mute the imported inflation effect.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, there are still signs emerging economies are running near full steam and that inflation could persist should domestic demand hold up in the face of slowdowns in the U.S. and Europe. Hong Kong, Brazil and South Korea reported strong jobs numbers this week, with Hong Kong's unemployment rate hitting 3.2% in the three months ended August, its lowest point since the Asian financial crisis in early 1998. Hong Kong said Thursday its consumer prices in August rose 5.7% in August, down from July's 7.9% but still considered elevated.\par \par \uc2 "Inflation is worryingly sticky," Frederic Neumann, economist for \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in Hong Kong, recently wrote. "There is no clear evidence that price pressures are easing." He notes that core inflation in China, which excludes volatile food and energy, is at a 14-year high.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Neumann says the usual tonic for inflation -- slower growth -- won't help as much in Asia this time because of the way Asia's economies have shifted in recent years. There's less empty manufacturing capacity than in previous cycles. And the shift toward long-term construction-related investment as a growth driver means price pressures will persist longer, as it takes time for projects to wind down. In the past, a drop in exports meant companies would simply stop producing goods, relieving price and wage pressures.\par \par \uc2 One solution to the falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  problem is for central banks to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. But the effect of those \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be limited when markets experience massive capital flow reversals, as is currently occurring. Indonesia, South Korea and Brazil have all \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent days, but the effect has been more to slow depreciation somewhat, not stop it or reverse the trend. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com }}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 23, 2011 04:53 ET (08:53 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110923e79n0025z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
