\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2 's Mukherjee Sees Rupee  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Coming As Needed\b0\par\par\uc2 302 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2011\par 23:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Updated to add more comments from Mukherjee beginning in sixth paragraph.)\par \par \uc2 By Anjali Cordeiro\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--India's policy makers are keeping an eye on the drop in the rupee and the volatility of capital flows and will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the situation warrants, \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current account can absorb the present degree of volatility in investment flows, Finance Minister Pranab Mukherjee told reporters Friday.\par \par \uc2 "But if it goes beyond a point and if we find we don't have that absorption capacity, then we shall have to take steps. That stage has not come," he said about measures to manage capital flows.\par \par \uc2 Already, the risk aversion in global markets has driven the Indian rupee sharply lower in recent days, but it recovered somewhat Friday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling dollars.\par \par \uc2 "We will watch the situation," Mukherjee said about the fall in the rupee. "As and when \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be required that will be considered at that stage."\par \par \uc2 Mukherjee said that volatile capital flows and swings in commodity prices are worsening the situation for emerging markets. Mukherjee, who is in Washington for the International Monetary Fund meetings, said he had a discussion with the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  head, also in the city, about the market volatility.\par \par \uc2 Loose monetary policy and low interest rates in the developed world have driven a tsunami of money into the developing world over the last year and driven up emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But as risk aversion has escalated over the last few weeks due to worries about the euro zone's debt crisis, the bullish sentiment on emerging markets has reversed.\par \par \uc2 -Anjali Cordeiro, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2200 [ 09-23-11 1726ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110923e79n000tx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
