\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Reserve Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  Likely  \highlight22\uc2 Intervened \highlight\uc2  In  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Market - Dealers\b0\par\par\uc2 569 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2011\par 13:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- RBI likely to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars, according to state-run bank traders\par \par \uc2 -- Suspected action likely to have been to curb volatility rather than floor the rupee's fall, say traders\par \par \uc2 -- Rupee may stay weak, dollar may rise as high as INR52.18, says Standard Chartered analyst\par \par \uc2 (Recasts first paragraph, adds traders' comments in the 3rd paragraph, context in the 4th, 6th, 8th paragraphs, analyst's comments in the 5th paragraph, RBI official's comments in the 7th paragraph)\par \par \uc2 By Nupur Acharya and Bijou George\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee reversed early gains against the U.S. dollar in intraday trading Friday, following a suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , two dealers at state-run banks said, a sign the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  has finally stepped in to stem a free-fall of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which shed about 5% against the greenback over past 10 trading sessions.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the dollar was around INR49.85 by selling the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sending it to an intraday low of INR49.09, one of the dealers told Dow Jones Newswires. The fall was accelerated "as local stocks too recouped some losses from the early session," he said. Both dealers didn't want to be named.\par \par \uc2 Both traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to have been very large, although neither indicated a specific volume. However, with the market betting heavily on yet another dollar rally, it wouldn't take more than a few hundred million dollars to spark a sharp realignment of trading positions to trigger the dollar's fall following the RBI move, traders said.\par \par \uc2 The suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes after the rupee fell to a more than 28-month low against the dollar, in what some analysts suggested could be the beginning of a fall to its post-Lehman lows. The rupee's fall comes as risk-sensitive assets across the world take a beating, after widespread disappointment from a U.S. Federal Reserve decision Wednesday to tinker with the maturity profile of its bond portfolio, rather than expand its buyback program.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Priyanka Kishore at Standard Chartered Bank expects the dollar to rise to as much as INR52.18, given the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's indication earlier Friday that it would only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb volatility in the exchange rate. The rupee fell more than 2.5% on Thursday in what traders say was the sharpest single-day fall in more than a year.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  releases lagged data reflecting \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . According to the latest data, it has stayed on the sidelines since July.\par \par \uc2 Earlier Friday, RBI deputy governor Subir Gokarn told television channel CNBC TV 18 the bank wouldn't look to protect a fixed exchange rate, and would only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to smooth volatility. Volatility in the exchange rate was "part of the game", Gokarn said, in comments that Standard Chartered said indicated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would stick to minimal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at best.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was last trading at INR49.41 after climbing to a peak of INR49.89 during the day, compared with INR49.57 late Thursday.\par \par \uc2 An RBI spokesperson wasn't immediately available for comment.\par \par \uc2 -By Nupur Acharya and Bijou George, Dow Jones Newswires; +91-22-61456117; nupur.acharya@dowjones.com [ 23-09-11 1106GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110923e79n000qw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
