\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 2-Emerging central banks step up  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  support\b0\par\par\uc2 1,050 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2011\par 12:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * S.Korea sells $4 bln, pulls won up 1.2 percent\par \par \uc2 * India \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says not targeting \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate\par \par \uc2 * Brazil says monitoring \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  effect on inflation\par \par \uc2 (Recasts, adds details on Brazil, China, Switzerland)\par \par \uc2 By Suvashree Dey Choudhury and Kim Yeonhee\par \par \uc2 Sept 23 (Reuters) - Central banks in Asia and Latin America redoubled efforts to defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week as fears of slower global growth spurred investors to pull money out of emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 Anxiety rose after the Federal Reserve delivered a gloomy prognosis for the U.S. economy, spooking markets already on edge over a possible Greek default and euro zone bank crisis.\par \par \uc2 Investors responded by seeking safety in U.S. Treasuries and snapping up dollars, a move that pushed \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from India, Brazil and elsewhere to multi-year lows.\par \par \uc2 South Korea stepped up its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lift the won from its weakest level in a year Friday, though the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was still headed for its biggest weekly loss since early 2009.\par \par \uc2 Central banks in Thailand and the Philippines also waded into the market this week while Indonesia went further and bought long-term bonds. India said Friday it was merely trying to calm volatile trade by buying the rupee, which hit a 28-month low against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, emerging markets were fretting about excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength as investors seeking returns that were higher than what struggling developed markets could offer poured money into local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , stocks and bonds.\par \par \uc2 But while a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can hurt exports, a sudden decline threatens to worsen inflationary pressure in these economies, which have been growing at a faster clip than developed economies in the United States, Europe and Japan.\par \par \uc2 Alexandre Tombini, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief, said officials were monitoring markets to assess the impact of a weaker real on inflation. [ID:nS1E78M0FC]\par \par \uc2 Brazil sold $2.75 billion of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps on Thursday, an action that bolsters the real by mimicking purchases of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the futures market. The real <BRLCLOSE=> closed at a two-year low against the dollar earlier this week but was up some 2 percent on Friday <BRBY>.\par \par \uc2 "I think central banks are reluctant to let the market dictate \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  direction completely and they are very fearful that in times like these the market moves can become very one-sided as everybody rushes to the exit," said Jonathan Cavenagh, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Westpac Institutional Bank in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think that they are trying to alter the current trends but prevent an unwind of positioning like what we saw during the Asian financial crisis."\par \par \uc2 Taiwan unloaded $300 million to prop up the Taiwan dollar on Friday <TWD=> a day after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  posted its biggest one-day fall in 10 years.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  faced criticism from the island's powerful exporters over its strength.\par \par \uc2 Korea's won has lost 10.6 percent against the dollar so far this month. The Indian rupee and Indonesian rupiah have shed between 6 and 7 percent each, while Brazil's real is down more than 15 percent since September began <BRL=>.\par \par \uc2 DEPENDING ON RESERVES\par \par \uc2 Central banks have adopted different approaches to the problem, though. Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the swaps market rather than selling dollars outright means it did not have to deplete its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  placed $181 million in deposit certificates to halt the sol's decline <PEN=>.\par \par \uc2 Central banks can more easily weaken a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because they have the power to print more; defending an exchange rate is limited by the amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves on hand.\par \par \uc2 That's not a problem for many Asian central banks, which have reserve stockpiles running into the billions and, in China's case, trillions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 But sources at the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  said authorities were reluctant to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more aggressively because its reserves are limited.\par \par \uc2 "If we do \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at all, it will be with a very narrow objective of smoothing what might be a very volatile market situation, nothing beyond that," Deputy RBI Governor Subir Gokarn told a television channel in India on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The rupee has been the worst performer among major Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of late, hitting a two-year low against the dollar this week. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has pushed up interest rates aggressively in an attempt to counter inflation.\par \par \uc2 Traders estimated the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities in Korea dumped about $4 billion in dollars on Friday, helping the won gain to 1,166.0 per dollar from 1,179.8 on Thursday.<KRW=KFTC>\par \par \uc2 China's tightly controlled renminbi also fell against the dollar in the offshore market Friday as investors bet Beijing would slow recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains as the global outlook worsens. [ID:nL3E7KN0WK]\par \par \uc2 The spot renminbi trades in a narrow band against the dollar but China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had repeatedly set its midpoint at record highs over the last month to help counter inflation.\par \par \uc2 Some investors fear that may stop if growth slows, recalling China's attempt to freeze the yuan against the dollar in the aftermath of the 2008 financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 A DIFFERENT CHALLENGE\par \par \uc2 Developed economies such as Japan and Switzerland are facing an altogether different problem. Both are value as safe havens by global investors in times of trouble, which puts unwanted upward pressure on their exchange rates.\par \par \uc2 Unlike emerging markets, the Japanese and Swiss economies are struggling, and strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  hurt export and tourism industries and threaten to weaken growth even more.\par \par \uc2 The Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has responded by selling enough francs to maintain a target of 1.20 per euro, well off a record high of 1.0075 hit in August <EURCHF=>.\par \par \uc2 Japan also interviewed in August as the yen soared to a record high against the dollar, and with the yen still hovering near its all-time high, markets fear another strike.\par \par \uc2 "We've been saying all along that we will take decisive action against speculative, excessive yen rises that deviate from economic fundamentals," Finance Minister Jun Azumi said Thursday in Washington, where G20 leaders gathered.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Jonathan Standing in Taiwan; Leika Kihara in Washington DC; Writing by Steven C. Johnson and Kavita Chandran; Editing by Chizu Nomiyama)\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 / (WRAPUP 2)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110926e79n000no\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
