\par\par \b \uc2 India Rupee Recovers From 28-Month Low On Suspected RBI Action\b0\par\par\uc2 470 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2011\par 14:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                             As Of 1200 GMT \par                          Latest      Change \par    USD/INR               49.43       -0.14 \par    7.80% 2021 Bond       8.30%       Unchanged \par    Call Rate             7.25%       -105 BPs \par    Forward Dollar Premium/Discount* \par    Aug                   1.275       +0.045 \par    *Forward Dollar Premium/Discount are midpoints of bid-offer spreads \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee recovered from a fresh 28-month low against the U.S. dollar Friday, after a suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by selling the greenback, but the recovery was halted as global risk aversion propelled some dollar buying again late in the day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The dollar was trading at INR49.43 late in the session, compared with INR49.57 late Thursday. The dollar rose to INR49.89 intraday, its highest level since May 14, 2009.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the dollar was around INR49.85 by selling the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sending it to an intraday low of INR49.09, a dealer told Dow Jones Newswires. Another dealer confirmed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action.\par \par \uc2 The move shows the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  has finally stepped in to stem a free-fall for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that shed about 5% against the greenback over past 10 trading sessions.\par \par \uc2 Still, the market believed the RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was limited to curbing the volatility and wasn't intended to boost the rupee, in-line with the stance \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Deputy Governor Subir Gokarn outlined during the day.\par \par \uc2 "At this point, we don't see an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from a rate-targeting view point as that would reflect a change in the policy stance," he told a television channel.\par \par \uc2 Gokarn said the current situation is different from the economic crisis of 2008. "The macro environment is confusing, it's turbulent, but the causes of it are slightly different. So it is going to be wait and watch."\par \par \uc2 He said a "knee-jerk reaction" may lead to greater confusion.\par \par \uc2 Priyanka Kishore, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Standard Chartered Bank, said Gokarn's comments flag the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's minimal-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance.\par \par \uc2 Gokarn's comments could gear the market up for the dollar to move toward its post-Lehman high of INR52.18, Kishore added.\par \par \uc2 In the sovereign debt market, bonds ended almost unchanged, as investments into bonds due to global risk aversion were offset by concerns of higher government borrowing in the October-March period.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark 7.80% 2021 bond ended the session at INR96.74, compared with Thursday's INR96.75.\par \par \uc2 "The sharp depreciation of the rupee can increase India's import bill significantly and may force the government to borrow more from the market," a dealer at a state-run bank said.\par \par \uc2 -By Sourav Mishra, Dow Jones Newswires; +91-22-61456112; sourav.mishra@dowjones.com [ 23-09-11 1217GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110923e79n000u1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
