\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee hits 2-year low on euro woes\b0\par\par\uc2 480 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2011\par 04:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Italy's ratings downgrade worsens euro zone troubles\par \par \uc2 * Euro nears 7-month low, dollar index climbs toward 7-month peak\par \par \uc2 * Rupee may hit 48.50/dlr if cbank does not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  - traders\par \par \uc2 (Updates to mid-morning)\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Sept 20 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee fell to its lowest in two years on Tuesday as investors plumbed for the dollar's safety after rating agency S&P downgraded Italy, deepening fears over Europe's sovereign debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 The market is largely tracking the euro's slide and all eyes were on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for any likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said.\par \par \uc2 At 10:45 a.m. (0515 GMT), the partially convertible rupee\par \par \uc2 was at 48.13/14 per dollar, 0.7 percent weaker than Monday's close of 47.815/825. It had hit 48.24, a level last seen Sept. 25, 2009, in early trade.\par \par \uc2 "The dollar jumped like anything after the euro zone situation went from bad to worse. So an adverse reaction in the rupee was inescapable," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at a private sector bank.\par \par \uc2 For a story on the S&P action, see\par \par \uc2 "If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  steps in, then we can expect some retracement to 48. Otherwise, 48.50 looks plausible," the dealer said.\par \par \uc2 The rupee had last hit 48.50 on Sept. 15, 2009.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  is said to have last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last Wednedsay when the rupee had touched 48.02.\par \par \uc2 Dollar sales by exporters could help limit the rupee's fall, traders said.\par \par \uc2 The euro slid sharply on Tuesday against the dollar after Standard and Poor's cut its debt rating on Italy and as investors fretted over whether Greece can borrow badly needed cash from international lenders.\par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at $1.3617, moving closer to a seven-month low of $1.3495 touched last week, while the dollar index rose to 77.358 points, within the striking distance of the 7-month peak of 77.784 hit last week.\par \par \uc2 The euro had been at $1.3669 and the dollar index at 77.082 points at the end of rupee trade on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The one-month onshore forward premium was at 15.25 points from 13.75 on Monday, the three-month was at 45 points from 43 and the one-year was at 104.75 points from 104.25.\par \par \uc2 The one-month offshore non-deliverable forward contracts\par \par \uc2 were quoted at 48.38, weaker than the onshore spot rate.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures market , the most traded near-month dollar-rupee contracts on the National Stock Exchange, the MCX-SX and the United Stock Exchange were all at 48.2050. The total volume was $1.99 billion.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Aditya Phatak; Editing by Ranjit Gangadharan)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE/  (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110923e79k0003p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
