\par\par \b \uc2 RPT- \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  cbank did not  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  market in July\b0\par\par\uc2 235 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2011\par 04:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats story issued late on Tuesday)\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Sept 13 (Reuters) - India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not buy or sell dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in July, for the eight successive month, continuing with its hands-off approach, its monthly bulletin showed on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India (RBI) \uc2 has always maintained that it does not target any specific exchange rate and only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent excessive volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 The last time the RBI had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in November when it had bought $1.37 billion and sold $500 million, compared with purchases of $450 million in October.\par \par \uc2 On a net basis, that was the RBI's biggest monthly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since June 2009, when it had bought $1.04 billion.\par \par \uc2 The rupee moved in a band of 43.8550 to 44.76 per dollar in July.\par \par \uc2 In 2010, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought a net $1.85 billion, compared with net sales of $5.8 billion in 2009 when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the rupee from depreciating sharply.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the partially convertible rupee ended at 47.59/60 to a dollar, after touching 47.6550, its lowest since May 25, 2010, and 0.8 percent weaker from Monday's close of 47.22/23.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Swati Bhat; editing by Malini Menon)\par