\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK:China Bonds Tipped Up;US Debt Problems Under Scrutiny\b0\par\par\uc2 1,748 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 2011\par 01:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2330 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds are tipped to rise a touch Monday as demand from foreign investors is likely to increase after S&P cut the U.S. government debt rating to AA+ from AAA, says Adam Chen, a Beijing-based bond analyst with Hong Yuan Securities. "The U.S. rating downgrade may trigger some investors to reallocate their assets. As China is the world's second largest economy with remarkable economic growth, the country's government bonds are expected to be increasingly attractive." Still, he expects the imminent boost by the U.S. rating cut on domestic bonds may be limited given foreign investors' tiny presence in the local market. Only a few central banks and foreign lenders have been approved by the PBOC to participate in China's interbank market. (ming.wong@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2330 GMT [Dow Jones] LME base metals are likely to open weaker as investors shun risky assets following S&P's downgrade of the U.S. government's debt rating. Industrial metals, used widely in construction and manufacturing, were under pressure last week as investors became increasingly uneasy over the global economic outlook and sovereign debt problems in the euro-zone. LME 3-month copper ended down 3.4% at $9,040/ton, its lowest close since late June. Zinc ended 5.6% lower at $2,199.50/ton, with all the metals in the complex closing in negative territory. Base metals are hypersensitive to macro developments and fluctuations in the wider market mood. Base metals ignored Friday's better-than-expected U.S. non-farm payrolls, a key indicator of the health of the economy. (arpan.mukherjee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2329 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/INR is likely to open sharply lower after \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  downgraded the U.S. government's AAA sovereign credit rating Friday, say traders. "We have a tricky situation in India. While the rupee is likely to appreciate tracking the global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , an immediate sell off in local stocks may halt its rally," says Pramit Brahmbhatt, Chief Executive at Alpari Financial Services (\b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 ), a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  broker and advisor. He expects a soft \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the rupee appreciates sharply by 80 paisa to 90 paisa, but "it (the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) will be a very cautious action and case specific." He tips the pair to be around 44.50 or lower depending on global cues versus 44.73 late Friday. Two \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers expect the pair to trade in a 44.00- 44.80 band Monday. Standard Chartered Bank in a report last week rated INR 'overweight' and expected the pair to touch 43 levels in the short-term. (Sourav.Mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2328 GMT [Dow Jones] Friday's Hong Kong short-selling volume more than doubled Thursday's, at HK$11.82 billion, representing 9.5% of total market volume, indicating bears were relentless and expected more short-term market downside amid the HSI's 4.3% tumble Friday. Leveraged market proxy HKEx (0388.HK) led with HK$920.97 million, followed by \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) with HK$911.70 million, Tracker Fund (2800.HK) with HK$816.66 million. Tencent (0700.HK) and \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) rounded out top five, with HK$526.90 million and HK$421.76 million respectively. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2328 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares may continue to fall Monday on worries that S&P's downgrade of U.S. debt rating may further add to selling pressure in the U.S. markets. Traders expect the main index to find support at 3880 after it ended down 4.9% Friday at 3921.64. "I, however, expect that selloff here (in Indonesia) won't be as heavy as Friday as the Indonesian economy is sound and stronger than when it was hit by the 2008 crisis," says an analyst with PT Garuda Nusantara Capital. He expects the selloff will be brief and a steady IDR will likely give a support for a rebound. Commodity-related stocks which account for almost 30% of total market capitalization may lead falls on lower oil prices. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2327 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi may fall in the wake of the U.S. credit ratings downgrade, with immediate support for the index tipped at Friday's low of 1920 and then around 1880; a volatile session is expected with continued bargain hunting expected from local pension funds, following the index's whopping 10.5% slide during the past four sessions, analysts say. "(I) do think that stocks are significantly undervalued. But it'll take some time," for this to sink in given the current poor sentiment, say \uc2 Tong Yang Securities\uc2  analyst Lee Jae-mahn. The Kospi ended down 3.7% at 1943.75 Friday. But analysts note investors should take a more cautious approach for the long term, as the credit ratings downgrade had been expected for some time now, while South Korean Vice Finance Minister Yim Jong-yong on Sunday said there was little risk of another global economic slowdown despite heightened concerns about the U.S. and European economic health. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2327 GMT [Dow Jones] Major Asian, European equity markets are likely to open down 3% on high volatility after S&P cut the U.S. credit rating, says GFT director of global dealing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  Martin Slaney. "The downgrade on Saturday was far from unexpected, but I think the general feeling amongst traders was that it would not come for a few months. For S&P to make the announcement at the weekend should in theory allow some time for traders to absorb the news prior to the Monday open. But the likelihood of a global double-dip recession just became all the more real and we are in unchartered territory here." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2326 GMT [Dow Jones] Indian stocks will likely take cues from global markets early Monday, but they are unlikely to fall sharply following S&P's downgrade of U.S. debt, as they have already corrected in the previous week, says Ajay Parmar, head of research for institutional securities at Emkay Share & Stock Brokers. However, this is a "negative development" and the Sensex could "grind lower" in the short-term, given that India also faces high inflation and concerns over slowing economic growth, he says. The Sensex closed 2.2% lower at 17,305.87 on Friday.(sudeep.jain@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2326 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/SGD may be choppy Monday but is likely to resume its downward drift later in the week, Thomas Harr, head of Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Standard Chartered Bank says after S&P downgraded the U.S. sovereign credit rating late Friday. "The markets are mixed and our base case is that the brief dollar rally against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been stemmed. The...announcement by the ECB that it's starting to buy euro area bonds is positive for risk," Harr says. He adds that the U.S. dollar may resume its weakening trend against its Asian counterparts in 2-3 days. He tips 1.2180 resistance for USD/SGD Monday, with 1.2120 support. The pair is at 1.2140, off its 1.2093 early morning low and vs 1.2205 late Friday in Asia. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2325 GMT [Dow Jones] There is likely to be a "knee-jerk reaction" lower in Indian stocks Monday to S&P's downgrade of U.S. debt, but the development is a positive for Indian and other emerging market equities from a long-term perspective, says Mohit Mirchandani, head of portfolio management services at Religare Mutual Fund. "In times of risk aversion, money would flow out of emerging markets into U.S. Treasuries, but that safe haven is not so safe any more and there is no other asset class that can replace it immediately," he says. However, excess liquidity could also flow into commodities, which is negative for Indian markets as it would stoke inflationary pressures, he adds. The Sensex closed 2.2% lower at 17,305.87 on Friday. (sudeep. Jain@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2325 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold touches a new high in the opening minutes of Asian trade and rises by more than 1%. Investors flock to the safety of the yellow metal after S&P downgraded the U.S. government's AAA sovereign credit rating by one notch with a negative outlook. Spot gold hit $1,691.30/oz, a new record, in the first 20 minutes of trade in Asia, according to data from Future Source. Analysts expect a slide in global stock markets to weigh on prices of the yellow metal as traders cash out to meet margin calls. However, S&P's downgrade of the U.S. debt rating helping to push up gold as investor concerns deepened over the outlook of the world's largest economy. Spot gold is at $1,688.20/oz, up $24.80, silver is at $38.69/oz, up 37 cents, platinum is at $1,718/oz, up $2 while palladium is at $718/oz, down $24 from New York's informal close, according to kitco.com. (arpan.mukherjee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2323 GMT [Dow Jones] Indian government bonds are likely to gain in the short-term on expectations that a possible US economic slowdown will cool global commodity prices and ease inflationary pressures, says Mahendra Jajoo, executive director and chief investment officer at Pramerica Asset Managers. He says Indian paper may attract foreign-fund inflows following S&P's cut of U.S. debt rating; still, the impact may be limited as "unlike \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and equity markets, domestic bonds will be governed more by domestic factors like fiscal deficit target and debt supplies in the market in the medium-term," Jajoo says. He expects the benchmark 7.80%2021 bond yield to trade in a 7.28%-7.35% band during the session vs 8.31% at Friday's close. Traders said focus will also be on RBI sale of INR60 billion worth of cash management bills Monday. (Sourav.Mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 07, 2011 19:30 ET (23:30 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110807e78700309\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
