\par\par \b \uc2 Indian cbank did not  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 fx \highlight\uc2  market in Feb\b0\par\par\uc2 338 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2011\par 14:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, April 11 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not buy or sell dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in February, the third absence since November when it had bought a net $870 million, its monthly bulletin showed.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India (RBI) \uc2 had bought $1.37 billion and sold $500 million in November, compared with purchases of $450 million in October.\par \par \uc2 On a net basis, November was the RBI's biggest monthly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since June 2009, when it had bought $1.04 billion.\par \par \uc2 In the current fiscal year that began last April, the bank has so far purchased a net $1.69 billion from the market.\par \par \uc2 In February, the rupee moved in wide band of 44.9850 to 45.87 per dollar, closely mirroring moves in the domestic sharemarket and reducing the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb excessive volatility or movement in either direction.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't target a specific level for the rupee and has said it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market only to prevent excessive volatility.\par \par \uc2 In 2010, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought a net $1.85 billion compared with net sales of $5.8 billion in 2009, when it had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the rupee from depreciating sharply.\par \par \uc2 The rupee had dropped to a record low of 52.2 per dollar in March 2009, but ended the year 4.7 percent higher, boosted by foreign fund inflows of $17.5 billion. In 2010, the rupee rose 4.1 percent, boosted by record inflows of $29.3 billion.\par \par \uc2 Foreigners have poured more than $3 billion into equities after being net sellers in the first two months of 2011.\par \par \uc2 The partially convertible rupee ended at 44.3850/3950 per dollar on Monday, 0.72 percent weaker from Friday's close of 44.07/08 when it had hit 43.98, a level not seen since Oct. 15.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Neha D'silva; Editing by Aradhana Aravindan)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INDIA\b0 \highlight\uc2 -CBANK-\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 /   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110411e74b0010y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
