\par\par \b \uc2 ADB Official: Expect Indian Rupee To Be Stable This FY\b0\par\par\uc2 By Prasanta Sahu   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 363 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2011\par 09:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Asian Equities Report\par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee is likely to be stable through the current fiscal year that began April 1, with capital inflows helping to comfortably manage the country's large current account deficit, an Asian Development Bank official said.\par \par \uc2 "We have a stable outlook for the rupee," Rana Hasan, principal economist at the India office of the Asian Development Bank, told Dow Jones Newswires late Wednesday in an interview.\par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained 1.5% against the U.S. dollar in the last fiscal year. It gained 0.8% so far since April 1 and was last trading at INR44.20 to a dollar.\par \par \uc2 India's October-December current account deficit sharply narrowed to $9.7 billion from $12.2 billion a year earlier as non-trade payments such as receipts for software services increased, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed.\par \par \uc2 But it still remains high compared with its emerging market peers, most of which run a surplus. The wide current account gap has been compounded by a large fiscal deficit.\par \par \uc2 The ADB expects India's current account deficit to widen to 3.5% in the current fiscal year from about 3% last fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 Capital inflows--into both equity and debt--amounted to $32.2 billion in the last fiscal year and the government expects them to remain below $40 billion this fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 Hasan said the rupee's current levels reflect the country's economic fundamentals as the exchange rate remains largely flexible.\par \par \uc2 "I think one of the benefits of having a flexible exchange rate is that the current exchange rate does reflect economic fundamentals," he said. "I think it [flexible exchange rate] has served \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  well."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in case of too much volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is appropriate for India, he said.\par \par \uc2 The economy's fundamentals aren't going to change dramatically, he said.\par \par \uc2 The ADB expects India's economy to grow 8.2% for this fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 -By Prasanta Sahu; Dow Jones Newswires; 91 11 43563309; subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com [ 07-04-11 0709GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument AER0000020110407e7470000p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
