\par\par \b \uc2 Indian cbank did not  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 fx \highlight\uc2  market in Jan\b0\par\par\uc2 330 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 05:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, March 11 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not buy or sell dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in January, the second absence since November when it had bought a net $870 million, its monthly bulletin showed.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India (RBI) \uc2 had bought $1.37 billion and sold $500 million in November, compared with purchases of $450 million in October.\par \par \uc2 On a net basis, November was the RBI's biggest monthly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since June 2009, when it had bought $1.04 billion.\par \par \uc2 In the current fiscal year that began last April, the bank has so far purchased a net $1.69 billion from the market.\par \par \uc2 In January, the rupee moved in wide band of 44.60 to 46.01 per dollar, closely mirroring moves in the domestic sharemarket and reducing the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb excessive volatility or movement in either direction.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't target a specific level for the rupee and has said it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market only to prevent excessive volatility.\par \par \uc2 In 2010, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought a net $1.85 billion compared with net sales of $5.8 billion in 2009, when it had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the rupee from depreciating sharply.\par \par \uc2 The rupee had dropped to a record low of 52.2 per dollar in March 2009, but ended the year 4.7 percent higher, boosted by foreign fund inflows of $17.5 billion. In 2010, the rupee rose 4.1 percent, boosted by record inflows of $29.3 billion.\par \par \uc2 So far in 2011, however, foreign funds are net sellers of $1.8 billion, pushing the rupee down over 1 percent this year.\par \par \uc2 At 0355 GMT on Friday, the partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> was trading at 45.23/24 per dollar, weaker than 45.18/19 at close on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Swati Bhat; Editing by Ranjit Gangadharan)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INDIA\b0 \highlight\uc2 -CBANK-\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 /   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110311e73b0006i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
