\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  cbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  the most in 17 months in Nov\b0\par\par\uc2 284 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2011\par 11:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Jan 12 (Reuters) - India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought a net $870 million from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in November, taking net purchases in 2010 to $1.85 billion, its monthly bulletin showed on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India (RBI) \uc2 bought $1.37 billion, while it sold $500 million in November compared with only purchases of $450 million in October.\par \par \uc2 On a net basis, it is the RBI's biggest monthly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since June 2009, when it had bought $1.04 billion.\par \par \uc2 In the current fiscal year that began in April 2010, the bank has so far purchased a net $1.69 billion from the market.\par \par \uc2 The rupee moved in a wide band of 44.1950 to 46.12 per dollar in November and dropped 3.2 percent on the month, its biggest monthly fall since May.\par \par \uc2 In 2009, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a net $5.8 billion to prevent the rupee from depreciating sharply. The rupee had dropped to a record low of 52.2 per dollar in March 2009, but ended the year 4.7 percent higher, boosted by foreign fund inflows of $17.5 billion.\par \par \uc2 Foreign funds bought a record $29.3 billion worth of shares in 2010 helping the rupee gain 4.1 percent. However, so far in 2011, they are net sellers of around $250.2 million.\par \par \uc2 At 3:58 p.m. (1028 GMT), the partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> was at 45.09/10 per dollar, stronger than 45.1625/1725 at close on Tuesday. (Reporting by Swati Bhat; editing by Malini Menon)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INDIA\b0 \highlight\uc2 -CBANK/\b \