\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ 2nd UPDATE: IMF Says India Growth To Stay Strong, Urges Reforms\b0\par\par\uc2 783 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 2011\par 09:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments from IMF official, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  economist) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jeffrey Sparshott & Prasanta Sahu \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--India's economy is expected to continue expanding rapidly in coming years, but authorities should raise interest rates to curb inflation and push ahead with fiscal and financial reforms to maintain that growth, the International Monetary Fund said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "Sustaining rapid growth over the medium term will depend on sustaining reforms to facilitate infrastructure investment," the IMF said in a report on India's economy.\par \par \uc2 India's economic growth was among the strongest in the world last year, making it one of the driving forces of the global recovery.\par \par \uc2 The IMF forecasts that India's gross domestic product will grow 8.75% this fiscal year, which runs through March. Growth will taper off to about 8% the following year, the IMF said.\par \par \uc2 But India also is grappling with high inflation, surging inflows of foreign capital and high levels of government debt.\par \par \uc2 The IMF recommended that India accelerate its plans to end economic stimulus measures adopted during the global economic crisis.\par \par \uc2 "More substantial current spending adjustment, mainly by further cutting subsidies and improving the targeting of spending, would help achieve social goals along with medium-term fiscal consolidation," the IMF said.\par \par \uc2 That includes liberalization of diesel and fertilizer prices, the IMF added.\par \par \uc2 Earlier Wednesday, Oil Minister Murli Deora said the government is trying to prevent further increases in the price of diesel amid rising crude oil costs. Diesel is the main transportation fuel in India, and higher prices mean higher freight costs for essential commodities such as food.\par \par \uc2 The IMF recommended that India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  further increase interest rates to tackle inflation.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  raised interest rates six times in 2010, and is widely expected to hike them again at its Jan. 25 policy meeting.\par \par \uc2 Real interest rates "are negative now. So rates should be moving to positive territory," Sanjaya Panth, IMF's senior resident representative to India, told reporters Thursday in New Delhi.\par \par \uc2 "We see room for further rate increases," but it's "something that has to be done gradually and that needs to be looked at continuously," Panth said.\par \par \uc2 The IMF expects headline inflation to be at 6.5% by end-March. That would be higher than the current policy interest rate, or repurchase rate, which stands at 6.25%.\par \par \uc2 The RBI's estimate is for 5.5% inflation at the end of March, with an upward risk to the forecast. The inflation rate was at 7.48% on-year in November, according to the most recent data.\par \par \uc2 Robert Prior-Wandesforde, head of India and South East Asia economics for \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 , said he endorsed the report's approach.\par \par \uc2 "A key issue is that real interest rates are negative," Prior-Wandesforde said. Monetary policy in India "is basically too loose for an economy that is growing above its sustainable rate."\par \par \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  forecasts another 75 basis points of rate hikes in 2011.\par \par \uc2 The IMF also cautioned Indian authorities on the country's current account deficit, which is expected to reach 3.3% of GDP in 2010-11.\par \par \uc2 "As the deficit rises, so does the potential impact of a sudden stop or reversal of capital flows. Another risk is that the scale of the inflows could exceed India's capacity to absorb them," the IMF said.\par \par \uc2 But India doesn't need any new capital controls for now, Panth said. India currently caps the amount of money foreigners can invest in local debt markets.\par \par \uc2 "We don't think India's absorptive capacity has been reached or exceeded," he said.\par \par \uc2 India's first line of defense against an oversized deficit is a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the IMF said. If the rupee became too strong, the international lender suggested \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market or take regulatory measures.\par \par \uc2 "Over time, deepening the corporate bond market, increasing the supply of shares and further liberalizing foreign direct investment would increase the country's capacity to absorb capital from abroad," the IMF said.\par \par \uc2 The IMF also said India needs to pursue reforms to encourage private investment in infrastructure development, such as deepening the corporate bond market and lowering the cost of doing business in the country.\par \par \uc2 "There are several obstacles to achieving set targets. These include availability of financing, land acquisition, multiple clearances, capacity constraints, and governance issues along with various sector-specific concerns," the IMF said.\par \par \uc2 -By Jeffrey Sparshott, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9291; jeffrey.sparshott@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Rakesh Sharma contributed to this article.)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 06, 2011 03:06 ET (08:06 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110106e716001du\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
