\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  cbank: bought $450 mln in October  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 362 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Dezember 2010\par 14:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's cbank net buyer of $975 mln in 2010\par \par \uc2 * Net dlr purchases by cbank in FY11 at $820 mln\par \par \uc2 (Updates with background, details)\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Dec 9 (Reuters) - India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had bought $450 million from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, while it did not sell any dollars in October, its monthly bulletin showed on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has so far this calendar year been a net buyer of dollars in the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, having bought a net $975 million. In the current fiscal year starting April, the bank has purchased a net $820 million from the market.\par \par \uc2 The rupee had risen to a 25-\u189\'00\'BD month high of 43.95 per dollar in October on the back of dollar inflows towards \uc2 Coal India\uc2 's initial public offering through which it raised $3.4 billion.\par \par \uc2 Earlier RBI bulletins had not shown any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since December 2009, but the latest issue showed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had sold $25 million in December, bought $155 million, $110 million and $260 million in March, June and September, respectively.\par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars around 44.10 rupees on Oct. 14, in a move that helped brake the rise in the rupee, sources told Reuters at that time. [ID:nSGE69D0EX]\par \par \uc2 In 2009, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a net $5.8 billion to prevent the rupee from depreciating sharply. The rupee had dropped to a record low of 52.2 per dollar in March 2009, but ended the year 4.7 percent higher, boosted by inflows of $17.5 billion.\par \par \uc2 Foreign funds had bought shares worth a net $5.6 billion in October, helping the rupee gain 1.1 percent. Their total purchases of local shares so far in 2010 now stand at a record $28.9 billion.\par \par \uc2 The partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> closed at 45.21/22 per dollar, weaker than 45.09/10 at close on Wednesday. The unit is up 2.9 percent on the year. (Reporting by Swati Bhat; Editing by Aradhana Aravindan)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INDIA\b0 \highlight\uc2 -CBANK/\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|SNI|SNS|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101209e6c9000wh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
