\par\par \b \uc2 MARKETWATCH VIEW: Rise In The Road Ahead\b0\par\par\uc2 By John Satish Kumar   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES COLUMN   \par \uc2 969 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 November 2010\par 05:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Emerging Markets Report\par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 What a difference a week makes. Just when it seemed that most macro risks were benign, and even the ones overseas had receded somewhat, came China's sudden spurt in inflation, the failure of the Group of 20 countries to hammer out anything substantial, and sovereign risks in Europe back in the spotlight.\par \par \uc2 India's benchmark Sensex closed 2.1% lower Friday, trading down 4% from the previous Friday's record close of 21,004.96 when it seemed right in the middle of a sweet spot.\par \par \uc2 The G-20 communique acknowledged that emerging economies could use macro-prudential tools to control capital inflows, pointing to capital controls to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation in check. India is probably the only emerging economy that has openly said that it won't put any caps on foreign-fund inflows, as it banks on these to contain the widening current account deficit.\par \par \uc2 India's current-account deficit widened to $13.7 billion in the April-June quarter from $4.5 billion a year earlier, with the gap marginally larger than the January-March quarter, when it reached $13 billion.\par \par \uc2 Global investors focused Friday on China's risks of rapid credit growth, asset bubbles and inflation, sending the Shanghai Composite Index 5.16% lower. But Columbia University's Nobel Prize winning economist Joseph Stiglitz last week argued that India is more vulnerable to an asset bubble than China is at this point.\par \par \uc2 "Where I do have a worry is countries like \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where they are debating how much \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market we should have. And there are people who say in India we shouldn't have capital controls, even though Brazil's done it, China's done it," Stiglitz warned.\par \par \uc2 He said that "strong economies that don't yet have capital control become the focal point" for the liquidity injected by the U.S. Federal Reserve. He probably has a point.\par \par \uc2 New Delhi has been happy with letting dollar inflows continue unabated. After all, along with local consumption, foreign capital inflows are the two engines driving growth in India.\par \par \uc2 Year-to-date, foreign funds have invested $28.69 billion in equities and $10.26 billion in bonds, according to data with the capital-market regulator.\par \par \uc2 To put these numbers in perspective, that is 28.3% of all dollar inflows into equities and a whopping 57.9% into bonds from the time India opened its doors to foreign investors back in November 1992.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, inflows into bonds have also been aided by India's decision in late September to double foreign investment limits in government securities to $10 billion from $5 billion, and increase the limit for corporate bonds to $20 billion from $15 billion.\par \par \uc2 Still, in a signal of India's growing tolerance for the cash gush that is pushing the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stocks higher, the chairman of the prime minister's Economic Advisory Council said last week that the Indian economy has the capacity to absorb capital inflows of up to $70 billion annually without government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The quantitative easing by the U.S. Fed may have implications in terms of capital inflows, but as I had indicated earlier, India's current account deficit will be around 3% of gross domestic product" this fiscal year through March 2011, C. Rangarajan said.\par \par \uc2 East Asian economies were also happy to let rampant capital inflows into their countries until the Asian crisis emerged in the middle of 1997.\par \par \uc2 There are other parallels as well: substantial current account deficits, rising inflation, appreciation in both asset prices and exchange rates, and booms in real estate. India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , just a week and a half back, pre-empted a bubble in mortgage lending by tightening lending norms.\par \par \uc2 What the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  hasn't done yet is stem the rupee's rise. It did, however, recently warn about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if capital flows are volatile. But \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also brings with it inflation.\par \par \uc2 Inflation for the month of September was 8.62%, well above the comfort level of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This, despite hikes in key policy rates six times this year.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2 , which was solely focused on inflation so far this year, is now in a conundrum.\par \par \uc2 Government data Friday showed that India's industrial production in September grew 4.4% from a year earlier, its slowest pace in 16 months. What must have the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  worried is that this reading was sharply below the 7.0% median estimate in a Dow Jones Newswires poll of 15 economists and far below the 15.2% rise in July.\par \par \uc2 Deputy Governor Subir Gokarn admitted as much, saying Saturday that industrial production data "may reflect the beginning of a slowing in the growth momentum, but until we see every indicator pointing in the same direction, it's difficult" to get a clear picture.\par \par \uc2 Adding to his worries is the fact that, despite a good monsoon, food prices haven't come off.\par \par \uc2 "For the time being, I will set it (food prices) as a risk and challenge to inflation management," he said.\par \par \uc2 Which now puts the onus back on inflation. Both the RBI and market participants will be closely watching if inflation is cooling in line with expectations.\par \par \uc2 India will report inflation data for October on Monday. A median poll of 15 economists expect the wholesale prices to be likely up by 8.50% from a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 So, while external factors will overhang on Indian markets in the near term, the domestic picture has taken a turn as well.\par \par \uc2 (John Satish Kumar writes for MarketWatch. He can be reached at +91-22-61456118 or by email at john.kumar@dowjones.com.) [ 15-11-10 0410GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument EM00000020101115e6bf0000b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
