\par\par \b \uc2 SNAPSHOT-Indian policy highlights on Wednesday, Nov 10\b0\par\par\uc2 580 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 2010\par 11:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  Nov 10 (Reuters) - Following are statements from Indian policymakers as well as the latest news and scheduled events.\par \par \uc2  * Denotes new entry.\par \par \uc2  * India sees no need to introduce capital controls on portfolio flows, the deputy chairman of the country's planning commission said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2  "I also do not think at the present moment there is any need to introduce any capital controls on portfolio flows," Montek Singh Ahluwalia told Reuters at the Group of 20 meeting in Seoul.\par \par \uc2  India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a statement issued late Tuesday said it has eased margin requirements for corporate debt repurchases to 10-15 percent from 25 percent earlier.\par \par \uc2  India's government is planning to make firms disclose the end-use of foreign investment inflows, The Economic Times reported on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2  A committee headed by Finance Minister Pranab Mukherjee has called for "full disclosure" of the use of foreign equity, the newspaper said.\par \par \uc2  Two government departments have cautioned against any move to rapidly open up India's retail sector to foreign direct investment (FDI), The Economic Times reported on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2  "India should tread cautiously by opening up the sector, if at all, gradually and analyzing the impact before opening it more. FDI less than 18 percent may be thought of," the report quoted from a discussion paper comment by the micro, small and medium enterprise ministry.\par \par \uc2  The communication and IT ministry has also cautioned against the move, the newspaper reported.\par \par \uc2  CONTEXT NEWS:\par \par \uc2  * Some economies fear a U.S. Federal Reserve move to buy $600 billion more in government bonds by June 2011 will lead to a surge of "hot money" inflows into emerging markets such as India, South Korea and Brazil.\par \par \uc2  India's response has been measured in comparison with other economies such as China. [ID:nTOE6A300V]\par \par \uc2  The \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it will only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if inflows turn "lumpy" or "volatile". [ID:nSGE69E0CM]\par \par \uc2  The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  move means borrowing against AAA rated bonds will require a 10-percent margin, AA+ rated bonds would need a 12-percent margin, while AA rated bonds will require a 15-percent margin.\par \par \uc2  Borrowers will now need to offer lesser amount of securities or margins to borrow against corporate bonds.\par \par \uc2  There is no mechanism to monitor the use of FDI by Indian firms once the inflows have been approved.\par \par \uc2  While domestic political hurdles have stalled the opening up of certain sectors to FDI, India has seen a sharp rise in portfolio inflows recently. [ID:nSGE69B0CY]\par \par \uc2  FDI inflows into India from January to June this year stood at $10.78 billion, down 18 percent from a year ago.\par \par \uc2  India's retail sector favours small stores, with 51 percent of FDI allowed only in the single-brand sector. Multi-brand retail is restricted to cash-and-carry or wholesale outlets.\par \par \uc2  Opening up the sector would ease massive supply bottlenecks that have contributed to keeping inflation stubbornly high in India, Asia's third-largest economy.\par \par \uc2  Opening up various sectors to greater amounts of FDI is an ongoing process in India, with many long-standing reforms delayed by domestic political deadlock. [ID:nSGE69J0CY]\par \par \uc2  SCHEDULED EVENTS THIS WEEK:\par \par \uc2  FRIDAY, Nov. 12\par \par \uc2  -------------------\par \par 